OAI_PAPER({"slug": "The-Blockwise-Alperin-Weight-Conjecture-September-23-2026", "title": "The Blockwise Alperin Weight Conjecture", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Blockwise-Alperin-Weight-Conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "202", "family_title": "The blockwise Alperin weight conjecture", "abstract_html": "We prove the numerical blockwise Alperin weight conjecture for every finite group and every prime <i>p</i>. For each <i>p</i>-block <i>B</i>, the number of irreducible Brauer characters in <i>B</i> equals the number of conjugacy classes of <i>B</i>-weights.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(p\\) be a prime, let \\(G\\) be a finite group, and let \\(k\\) be an algebraically closed field of characteristic \\(p\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(p\\) be a prime, let \\(G\\) be a finite group, and let \\(k\\) be an algebraically closed field of characteristic \\(p\\). A \\(p\\)-block \\(B\\) is a primitive central idempotent of \\(kG\\). Write \\(l(B)\\) for the number of simple \\(kG\\)-modules belonging to \\(B\\), equivalently the number of irreducible Brauer characters in \\(B\\).</p><p>A \\(p\\)-weight of \\(G\\) is a pair \\((Q,\\varphi)\\), where \\(Q\\leq G\\) is a possibly trivial \\(p\\)-subgroup and\n\\[\n\\varphi\\in\\operatorname{Irr}(N_G(Q)/Q),\n\\qquad\n\\varphi(1)_p=|N_G(Q)/Q|_p.\n\\]\nHere \\(a_p\\) denotes the largest power of \\(p\\) dividing a positive integer \\(a\\).</p><p>The block assignment is the following. For \\(H\\leq G\\), coefficient restriction is\n\\[\ns_H:Z(kG)\\longrightarrow Z(kH),\n\\qquad\n\\sum_{g\\in G}a_gg\\longmapsto\\sum_{h\\in H}a_hh.\n\\]\nIf \\(b\\) is the block of the inflation of \\(\\varphi\\) to \\(H=N_G(Q)\\), assign the weight to \\(B\\) when\n\\[\n\\lambda_b\\circ s_H=\\lambda_B,\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\lambda_c\\) is the residue character of the local factor of the center corresponding to a block \\(c\\).</p><p>Let \\(W_p(B)\\) be the set of simultaneous \\(G\\)-conjugacy classes of weights assigned to \\(B\\).</p><p>Then\n\\[\nl(B)=|W_p(B)|.\n\\]\nThe assertion includes \\(Q=1\\) and is an equality of cardinalities; it does not assert a canonical or equivariant correspondence.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 21 supplies the uniform divisibility hypothesis of Proposition 6. Therefore\n\\[\nl=(1+T)z\n\\]\nfor every finite group and central block sum. Taking \\((X,E)=(G,B)\\),\n\\[\nl(B)\n=\n\\sum_{\\substack{Q\\leq G\\\\Q\\text{ a }p\\text{-subgroup}\\\\\\text{up to }G\\text{-conjugacy}}}\nz(N_G(Q)/Q,B^Q).\n\\]\nProposition 2 identifies the summands, including \\(Q=1\\), with the conjugacy classes of weights assigned to \\(B\\) under the stated central-character convention. Thus\n\\[\nl(B)=|W_p(B)|.\n\\]\nProposition 1 transfers the result from \\(\\overline{\\mathbb F}_p\\) to every algebraically closed coefficient field of characteristic \\(p\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Corollary 1.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(p\\) be a prime, let \\(G\\) be a finite group, let \\(B\\) be a \\(p\\)-block with defect group \\(D\\), and let \\(b\\) be its Brauer correspondent in \\(N_G(D)\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(p\\) be a prime, let \\(G\\) be a finite group, let \\(B\\) be a \\(p\\)-block with defect group \\(D\\), and let \\(b\\) be its Brauer correspondent in \\(N_G(D)\\). Then\n\\[\nl(B)\\geq l(b),\n\\qquad\nD\\text{ abelian}\\ \\Longrightarrow\\ l(B)=l(b).\n\\]</p><p>For \\(P\\in\\operatorname{Syl}_p(G)\\),\n\\[\n|\\operatorname{IBr}_p(G)|\n\\geq\n|\\operatorname{IBr}_p(N_G(P))|.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(B_0\\) be the principal \\(p\\)-block of \\(G\\), and let \\(k_p(G)\\) count the conjugacy classes of \\(p\\)-power-order elements, including the identity. Then\n\\[\nk_p(G)=3\\ \\Longrightarrow\\ l(B_0)\\geq\\frac{p-1}{2},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\np\\mid |G|\n\\ \\Longrightarrow\\\nl(B_0)\\geq 2\\sqrt{p-1}+1-k_p(G).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The local inequality and the abelian-defect equality are Alperin's Consequences 1–2 [1], restated in Hung–Sambale–Tiep [22, Section 3]; Theorem 1 supplies their numerical weight-conjecture hypotheses.</p><p>Put \\(N=N_G(P)\\). Since \\(P\\) is a normal Sylow \\(p\\)-subgroup of \\(N\\), it is a defect group of every block of \\(N\\). Brauer's first main theorem matches the blocks of \\(N\\) with the maximal-defect blocks of \\(G\\). Summing the local inequality gives\n\\[\n|\\operatorname{IBr}_p(G)|\n\\geq\n\\sum_{B'\\in\\operatorname{Bl}_{p,\\max}(G)}l(B')\n\\geq\n\\sum_{b'\\in\\operatorname{Bl}_p(N)}l(b')\n=\n|\\operatorname{IBr}_p(N)|.\n\\]</p><p>Theorem 1 also supplies the only conjectural input in Hung–Sambale–Tiep [22, Propositions 3.1–3.2], which give the two principal-block bounds under their respective hypotheses.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "Theorem 5.1, Exact purely inseparable genus estimate", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fix a prime \\(p\\), a finite group \\(J\\) with \\(O_p(J)=1\\), and put \\(m=|J|\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix a prime \\(p\\), a finite group \\(J\\) with \\(O_p(J)=1\\), and put \\(m=|J|\\). Let \\(\\kappa=\\overline{\\mathbb F}_p\\). For \\(s\\geq1\\), put \\(n=p^s\\), choose an elliptic curve \\(A_0/\\kappa\\) and a point \\(P\\in A_0(\\kappa)\\) of exact order \\(n\\), and let\n\\[\nF'=\\kappa(A_0^n).\n\\]\nWrite \\(t_1,\\ldots,t_n\\in A_0(F')\\) for the generic projection points. Define\n\\[\n\\sigma(t_i)=t_{i+1}-P\n\\quad(i\\text{ modulo }n),\n\\qquad\nF_0=(F')^{\\langle\\sigma\\rangle}.\n\\]\nLet \\(E/F_0\\) be the genus-one curve obtained by descending \\(A_{0,F'}\\) under the semilinear action\n\\[\nx\\longmapsto \\sigma(x)+P.\n\\]\nThe divisor \\(\\sum_i[t_i]\\) descends to a finite étale divisor \\(B\\) on \\(E\\).</p><p>For a regular integral projective curve \\(C\\) over its stated ground field, set\n\\[\n\\chi(C)=\\chi(\\mathcal O_C),\n\\qquad\n\\nu(C)=-2\\chi(C).\n\\]\nSuch a curve is not presumed smooth or geometrically reduced.</p><p>Fix \\(h\\geq1\\), and suppose \\(s\\geq h\\) and\n\\[\np^{s-h}&gt;m\\bigl(h+\\log_p m\\bigr).\n\\]\nLet \\(F/F_0\\) be finite with \\([F:F_0]_p\\leq p^h\\), and let \\(Z\\) be the normalization of \\(E_F\\) in a finite purely inseparable extension of its function field of degree \\(d\\leq m\\). Choose a compositum \\(S=FF'\\).</p><p>Then \\(Z_S\\) is integral and regular. If \\(r_i\\) is the ramification index of \\(Z_S\\to E_S\\) at the unique point above \\(t_i\\), then, with \\(\\nu(Z)\\) computed over \\(F\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{\\nu(Z)}d\n\\geq\n\\sum_{i=1}^n\\left(1-\\frac1{r_i}\\right).\n\\]\nThe threshold is uniform in \\(F\\), \\(Z\\), and the tuple-class multiset used in the counting argument.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 11 proves regularity and integrality after the splitting extension and gives\n\\[\nA=\n\\nu(Z)-d\\sum_i\\left(1-\\frac1{r_i}\\right)\n\\geq\n-d(h+\\log_p d).\n\\]\nProposition 12 makes \\(A\\) a multiple of \\(N=p^{s-h}\\). Since \\(d\\leq m\\),\n\\[\nA\\geq-m(h+\\log_p m)&gt;-N.\n\\]\nA multiple of \\(N\\) strictly greater than \\(-N\\) is nonnegative. Hence\n\\[\n\\frac{\\nu(Z)}d\n\\geq\n\\sum_i\\left(1-\\frac1{r_i}\\right).\n\\]\nThe sufficient threshold involves only \\(p,h,m,s\\), giving the stated uniformity.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 4", "orig": "Corollary 8.1, Character-count and defect data", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(B\\) be a \\(p\\)-block of a finite group, let its defect group \\(D\\) have order \\(p^d\\), and write \\( k(B)=|\\operatorname{Irr}(B)|. \\) If \\(k(B)-l(B)=1\\), then the possible numerical data \\((p^d,l(B))\\) are exactly …", "statement": "<p>Let \\(B\\) be a \\(p\\)-block of a finite group, let its defect group \\(D\\) have order \\(p^d\\), and write\n\\[\nk(B)=|\\operatorname{Irr}(B)|.\n\\]\nIf \\(k(B)-l(B)=1\\), then the possible numerical data \\((p^d,l(B))\\) are exactly the following.</p><p>First, there are positive integers \\(n,m\\) such that\n\\[\nd=nm,\n\\]\nevery prime divisor of \\(n\\) divides \\(p^m-1\\), and\n\\[\n4\\mid n\\ \\Longrightarrow\\ 4\\mid p^m-1,\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\nl(B)=\\sum_{e\\mid n}\\frac{p^{em}-1}{ne}\\,J_2(n/e),\n\\qquad\nJ_2(t)=t^2\\prod_{\\substack{q\\mid t\\\\q\\text{ prime}}}(1-q^{-2}).\n\\]</p><p>Second, the exceptional pairs are\n\\[\n(p^d,l(B))\\in\n\\left\\{\n(5^2,7),(7^2,8),(11^2,9),(11^2,35),\n(23^2,88),(29^2,63),(59^2,261)\n\\right\\}.\n\\]</p><p>All listed data occur, and in every case \\(k(B)=l(B)+1\\). This classifies numerical data, not blocks up to isomorphism, Morita equivalence, or derived equivalence.</p>", "proof": "<p>Hung, Sambale, and Tiep [22, Theorem 3.3] prove precisely this numerical classification under the numerical weight conjecture for \\(B\\). Theorem 1 supplies that hypothesis. Their examples establishing occurrence of every listed value are already unconditional.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 5", "orig": "Corollary 8.2, Functorial Alperin weight identity", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(k\\) and \\(F\\) be algebraically closed fields of characteristics \\(p\\) and \\(0\\), respectively.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(k\\) and \\(F\\) be algebraically closed fields of characteristics \\(p\\) and \\(0\\), respectively. For finite groups \\(H,G\\), view a \\((kH,kG)\\)-bimodule as a \\(k[H\\times G]\\)-module by\n\\[\n(h,g)m=hmg^{-1}.\n\\]\nIt is a \\(p\\)-permutation bimodule if its restriction to a Sylow \\(p\\)-subgroup has a permuted basis. It is diagonal if it is also projective on both sides. Let \\(T^\\Delta(H,G)\\) be the split Grothendieck group of diagonal \\(p\\)-permutation bimodules.</p><p>The category of group–central-idempotent pairs has objects \\((H,c)\\), including \\(c=0\\), and morphisms given by \\(F\\)-linear combinations of diagonal \\(p\\)-permutation bimodule classes selected by the two idempotents. Composition is tensor product over the intermediate group algebra. Let \\(\\mathcal F B\\ell_{F,k}\\) be its category of \\(F\\)-linear functors to \\(F\\)-vector spaces.</p><p>Write\n\\[\nFT^\\Delta_{H,c}\n=\nFT^\\Delta(-,H)\\circ kHc\n\\]\nfor the corresponding direct summand of the representable functor, and let\n\\[\n[[H,c]]_F=[FT^\\Delta_{H,c}]\n\\]\nin the Grothendieck group \\(K_0(\\mathcal F B\\ell_{F,k})\\) of the finite-length subcategory. Let \\(S_{1,1,F}\\) be the simple functor indexed by the trivial pair and its trivial \\(F\\)-module.</p><p>Let \\(b\\) be a block idempotent of \\(kG\\). Let \\(\\mathcal S_p(G)\\) consist of all strict chains\n\\[\n\\sigma=(1=P_0&lt;P_1&lt;\\cdots&lt;P_n)\n\\]\nof \\(p\\)-subgroups, including \\((1)\\), and put\n\\[\n|\\sigma|=n,\n\\qquad\nG_\\sigma=\\bigcap_{i=0}^nN_G(P_i),\n\\qquad\nb_\\sigma=\\operatorname{Br}_{P_n}(b).\n\\]\nThe element \\(b_\\sigma\\) is a central idempotent of \\(kG_\\sigma\\), possibly zero or a sum of blocks.</p><p>Then\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\sigma\\in[G\\backslash\\mathcal S_p(G)]}\n(-1)^{|\\sigma|}[[G_\\sigma,b_\\sigma]]_F\n=\n\\begin{cases}\n[S_{1,1,F}],&amp;b\\text{ has defect zero},\\\\\n0,&amp;b\\text{ has positive defect}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The functor category is semisimple in characteristic zero [4, Section 2.5]. By Yoneda, a representable has finite-dimensional endomorphism space \\(FT^\\Delta(H,H)\\); semisimplicity therefore makes it finite length, since independent simple summands contribute independent projections. Its direct summands also have finite length, so the stated Grothendieck classes are defined.</p><p>The multiplicity of \\(S_{1,1,F}\\) in \\(FT^\\Delta_{H,c}\\) is \\(l(H,c)\\): evaluation at the trivial group gives the space spanned by indecomposable projective \\(kHc\\)-modules [4, proof of Theorem 3.4].</p><p>Boltje–Bouc–Yılmaz [4, Theorem 3.5(1)] prove that every other simple-functor multiplicity cancels in the displayed alternating chain sum. The sum is thus an integer multiple of \\([S_{1,1,F}]\\). Their Theorem 3.5(2), using the universal numerical blockwise weight conjecture through its chain formulation, determines the remaining coefficient. Theorem 1 supplies that universal numerical equality, giving coefficient one in defect zero and zero in positive defect.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Coefficient fields and scalar extension; original Section 2.1.", "statement": "<p>Coefficient fields and scalar extension; original Section 2.1.</p><p>Let \\(X\\) be a finite group, let \\(k\\) be algebraically closed of characteristic \\(p\\), and choose an embedding\n\\[\n\\kappa=\\overline{\\mathbb F}_p\\hookrightarrow k.\n\\]\nScalar extension from \\(\\kappa\\) to \\(k\\) preserves simple modules, primitive blocks, simple-module counts, and the central-character block assignments used for weights.</p><p>Moreover, there is a splitting \\(p\\)-modular system \\((K,\\mathcal O,\\kappa)\\), where \\(\\mathcal O\\) is a complete discrete valuation ring of characteristic-zero fraction field \\(K\\), residue field \\(\\kappa\\), and \\(K\\) splits every subgroup and subquotient needed in the calculations with \\(X\\).</p><p>The classical inputs used below are the paper's Foundations 1–13: Sylow theory and elementary finite-group actions; finite-dimensional algebra and ordinary-character theory; the stated commutative-algebra and field facts; valuation prolongation and complete-DVR finiteness; projective descent and finite-stage descent; finite normalization; regularity, smoothness, and properness; proper cohomology and ample section rings; differentials, adjunction, and duality; elliptic schemes and multiplication maps; complex covering theory and characteristic-zero Riemann–Hurwitz; and Noetherian Stein connectedness.</p>", "proof": "<p>The ideal\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rad}(\\kappa X)\\otimes_\\kappa k\n\\]\nis nilpotent. The quotient of \\(kX\\) by this ideal is the scalar extension of the split matrix product\n\\[\n\\kappa X/\\operatorname{rad}(\\kappa X),\n\\]\nand is again split semisimple. The ideal is therefore \\(\\operatorname{rad}(kX)\\), and simple modules correspond under scalar extension.</p><p>The center commutes with extension: it is the kernel of the finitely many maps \\(a\\mapsto ax-xa\\), and field extension preserves kernels. Each local factor of \\(Z(\\kappa X)\\) has nilpotent radical and residue \\(\\kappa\\); after extension it remains local with residue \\(k\\). Thus primitive blocks correspond and simple-module counts agree. Coefficient restriction, restriction to centralizers in the Brauer map, quotient maps for normal subgroups, and local residue characters all commute with extension. The block assignment is therefore preserved.</p><p>Starting with \\(\\mathbb Z_p\\), lift a tower of finite separable residue extensions exhausting \\(\\kappa\\), take the union, and complete it. This gives a complete DVR with residue \\(\\kappa\\). In an algebraic closure of its fraction field, choose matrix realizations of every ordinary irreducible representation of every subgroup of \\(X\\). Adjoining their finitely many matrix entries gives a finite splitting extension. Its valuation ring is again a complete DVR; its residue field remains \\(\\kappa\\), which is algebraically closed. Quotient representations inflate, so the same coefficients split the required subquotients.</p><p>For subsequent calculations, central idempotents lift uniquely in the center over the complete DVR. Integral central elements act on ordinary irreducibles by scalars in \\(\\mathcal O\\): these scalars lie in \\(K\\), satisfy monic integral equations, and \\(\\mathcal O\\) is integrally closed. Reduction of such a scalar is the residue character of the block containing the ordinary irreducible.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Normalizer projection, weights, and finite chain inversion; original Lemmas 3.2 and 3.4 and Definition 3.3.", "statement": "<p>Normalizer projection, weights, and finite chain inversion; original Lemmas 3.2 and 3.4 and Definition 3.3.</p><p>Fix a finite group \\(X\\), a splitting system \\((K,\\mathcal O,k)\\) as in Proposition 1, and a central idempotent \\(E\\in kX\\). Define\n\\[\nl(X,E)=\\#\\{\\text{simple }kX\\text{-modules selected by }E\\},\n\\]\n\\[\nk_{\\mathrm{ord}}(X,E)\n=\n\\#\\{\\text{ordinary irreducibles selected by the lift of }E\\},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nz(X,E)\n=\n\\#\\{\\chi\\text{ selected by the lift of }E:\n\\chi(1)_p=|X|_p\\}.\n\\]</p><p>For a \\(p\\)-subgroup \\(Q\\leq X\\), let\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Br}_Q:(kX)^Q\\longrightarrow kC_X(Q)\n\\]\nbe coefficient restriction, and let \\(\\pi_Q:kN_X(Q)\\to k[N_X(Q)/Q]\\) be the quotient map. Put\n\\[\nE^Q=\\pi_Q\\bigl(s_{N_X(Q)}(E)\\bigr)\n=\\pi_Q\\bigl(\\operatorname{Br}_Q(E)\\bigr).\n\\]\nFor a conjugation-invariant function \\(g\\) on such pairs, define\n\\[\n(Tg)(X,E)\n=\n\\sum_{\\substack{1&lt;Q\\leq X\\\\Q\\text{ a }p\\text{-subgroup}\\\\\\text{up to }X\\text{-conjugacy}}}\ng(N_X(Q)/Q,E^Q).\n\\]</p><p>A chain is\n\\[\nC=(1=Q_0&lt;Q_1&lt;\\cdots&lt;Q_r),\n\\qquad |C|=r,\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\nH_C=\\bigcap_{i=0}^rN_X(Q_i),\n\\qquad\nE_C=\\operatorname{Br}_{Q_r}(E).\n\\]\nIt is normal if every \\(Q_i\\) is normal in \\(Q_r\\). The chain \\((1)\\) is included.</p><p>Then \\(l=(1+T)z\\) universally is precisely the weight equality for central block sums, with the stated block assignment and \\(Q=1\\) included. It is equivalent to\n\\[\nz(X,E)\n=\n\\sum_{\\substack{C/X\\\\C\\text{ normal}}}\n(-1)^{|C|}l(H_C,E_C).\n\\tag{3.4}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 2.1 makes \\(E^Q\\) a central idempotent. Its selected defect-zero quotient characters are exactly the weights assigned to the blocks in \\(E\\): uniqueness of integral central-idempotent lifting identifies their selection with\n\\[\n\\lambda_b(\\operatorname{Br}_Q(E))\n=\n\\lambda_b(s_{N_X(Q)}(E))\n=\n1.\n\\]\nOnce \\(Q\\) is fixed up to \\(X\\)-conjugacy, its quotient characters have no further identification, because \\(N_X(Q)\\) acts on \\(N_X(Q)/Q\\) by inner automorphisms. The identity term in \\(1+T\\) accounts for \\(Q=1\\). Therefore the weight equality is \\(l=(1+T)z\\), and Lemma 2.2 gives its finite chain-inversion form.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.2, Normalizer projection", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the setting of Proposition 2, \\(\\operatorname{Br}_Q\\) is a unital algebra homomorphism.", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition 2, \\(\\operatorname{Br}_Q\\) is a unital algebra homomorphism. If\n\\[\nQ\\leq H\\leq N_X(Q),\n\\qquad\nC_X(Q)\\leq H,\n\\]\nthen, for \\(v\\in Z(kX)\\),\n\\[\n\\pi_Q(s_H(v))=\\pi_Q(\\operatorname{Br}_Q(v)),\n\\qquad\n\\lambda_b(s_H(v))=\\lambda_b(\\operatorname{Br}_Q(v))\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\nfor every block \\(b\\) of \\(H\\), where \\(\\pi_Q:kH\\to k[H/Q]\\).</p><p>In particular, \\(\\lambda_b\\circ s_H\\) is the central character of a unique block of \\(X\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(g\\in C_X(Q)\\), let \\(Q\\) conjugate the pairs \\((a,b)\\) with \\(ab=g\\). Coefficient products are constant on the orbits. Every nontrivial orbit has size divisible by \\(p\\); the fixed pairs are exactly those with \\(a,b\\in C_X(Q)\\). This proves multiplicativity, and the identity is preserved.</p><p>Each coset \\(hQ\\) is stable under conjugation by \\(Q\\), since \\(h\\in N_X(Q)\\). The same cancellation on coefficient sums over a coset proves the first identity in (3.2). The element \\(\\operatorname{Br}_Q(v)\\) belongs to \\(Z(kH)\\).</p><p>Every nonzero module for a finite \\(p\\)-group has nonzero invariants: choose a central element \\(z\\) of order \\(p\\); the nonzero kernel of \\(z-1\\), whose \\(p\\)th power is zero, is a module for the smaller group \\(Q/\\langle z\\rangle\\), and induction applies. For a simple \\(kH\\)-module, the \\(Q\\)-invariants are \\(H\\)-stable and hence constitute the whole module. Thus every simple factors through \\(H/Q\\), proving the second identity.</p><p>The composite with \\(\\operatorname{Br}_Q\\) is a unital algebra character of \\(Z(kX)\\). Such characters are precisely the residue characters of its local factors, one for each block.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.4, Finite chain inversion", "depth": 1, "snippet": "With the definitions in Proposition 2, the terms of \\(T^r l\\) are indexed, without extra multiplicities, by \\(X\\)-conjugacy classes of normal chains of length \\(r\\).", "statement": "<p>With the definitions in Proposition 2, the terms of \\(T^r l\\) are indexed, without extra multiplicities, by \\(X\\)-conjugacy classes of normal chains of length \\(r\\). The term associated to \\(C\\) is\n\\[\nl(H_C/Q_r,\\pi_{Q_r}(E_C))\n=\nl(H_C,E_C).\n\\]</p><p>Consequently the universal identity \\(l=(1+T)z\\) is equivalent to (3.4).</p>", "proof": "<p>After \\(r\\) steps the group and idempotent are\n\\[\nH_C/Q_r,\n\\qquad\n\\pi_{Q_r}(s_{H_C}(E))=\\pi_{Q_r}(E_C).\n\\]\nThe preimage \\(Q_{r+1}\\) of a nontrivial \\(p\\)-subgroup of \\(H_C/Q_r\\) is a \\(p\\)-group strictly containing \\(Q_r\\). It lies in \\(H_C\\), so normalizes every preceding \\(Q_i\\). Conversely, every normal one-step extension arises this way. Its normalizer has preimage\n\\[\nH_C\\cap N_X(Q_{r+1})=H_{C'}.\n\\]\nAfter fixing \\(C\\), conjugation is by its stabilizer \\(H_C\\), which is precisely quotient conjugation at the next step.</p><p>For the idempotent, write \\(E=\\sum_{x\\in X}e_xx\\), put \\(H'=H_{C'}\\), and choose representatives \\(A\\) for \\(Q_{r+1}/Q_r\\). At \\(hQ_{r+1}\\), restriction followed by quotient has coefficient\n\\[\n\\sum_{a\\in A}\\sum_{q\\in Q_r}e_{haq}\n=\n\\sum_{t\\in Q_{r+1}}e_{ht}.\n\\]\nThese are the coefficients of\n\\[\n\\pi_{Q_{r+1}}(s_{H'}(E))\n=\n\\pi_{Q_{r+1}}(E_{C'})\n\\]\nby Lemma 2.1. Every simple \\(kH_C\\)-module factors through \\(H_C/Q_r\\), preserving the selected idempotent.</p><p>Every transition in \\(T\\) strictly decreases the \\(p\\)-part of the group order. Thus\n\\[\n(1+T)^{-1}=\\sum_{r\\geq0}(-T)^r\n\\]\nis evaluation-wise finite, giving the equivalence.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Cocenters, subsections, and support; original Section 3.2.", "statement": "<p>Cocenters, subsections, and support; original Section 3.2.</p><p>Let \\(X\\), \\(k\\), \\(K\\), \\(\\mathcal O\\), and \\(E\\) be as in Proposition 2. For an algebra \\(A\\), write\n\\[\nV(A)=A/[A,A],\n\\]\nwhere \\([A,A]\\) is the linear span of commutators. Central elements act on \\(V(A)\\) by multiplication.</p><p>A section of \\(X\\) consists of the elements whose \\(p\\)-parts are conjugate to a fixed \\(p\\)-element.</p><p>The simple-module and ordinary-character counts are ranks of multiplication by \\(E\\) on, respectively, the \\(p'\\)-class span and the full cocenter. Moreover,\n\\[\nk_{\\mathrm{ord}}(X,E)\n=\n\\sum_{\\substack{[u]\\text{ an }X\\text{-class}\\\\u\\text{ a }p\\text{-element}}}\nl(C_X(u),\\operatorname{Br}_{\\langle u\\rangle}(E)).\n\\tag{3.5}\n\\]\nMultiplication by \\(E\\) preserves the section decompositions of both the cocenter and the center.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 3.1 identifies the required ranks. Lemma 3.2 decomposes the full rank section by section and establishes the support-preservation assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.5, Cocenter ranks", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(A\\) be a finite-dimensional algebra over the algebraically closed field \\(k\\) of characteristic \\(p\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(A\\) be a finite-dimensional algebra over the algebraically closed field \\(k\\) of characteristic \\(p\\). The map\n\\[\nF_A:V(A)\\longrightarrow V(A),\n\\qquad\n\\overline a\\longmapsto\\overline{a^p},\n\\]\nis well-defined and Frobenius semilinear. Its stable image has dimension equal to the number of simple \\(A\\)-modules, respecting summands selected by central idempotents.</p><p>For \\(A=kX\\), the stable image is the span of the \\(p'\\)-classes. The ranks of multiplication by \\(E\\) on this span and on all of \\(V(kX)\\) are \\(l(X,E)\\) and \\(k_{\\mathrm{ord}}(X,E)\\), respectively.</p>", "proof": "<p>In the expansion of \\((a+b)^p\\), cyclic rotations of a mixed word have the same cocenter class. Their orbits have size \\(p\\), leaving only \\(a^p\\) and \\(b^p\\). Thus the power operation is additive and Frobenius semilinear. Also\n\\[\n\\overline{(ab)^p}=\\overline{(ba)^p}\n\\]\nby cyclic rotation; additivity then makes the operation kill the linear commutator space.</p><p>Let \\(R=\\operatorname{rad}(A)\\). The kernel of \\(V(A)\\to V(A/R)\\) is represented by elements of \\(R\\): lift a commutator expression downstairs and subtract it upstairs. A sufficiently high iterate of \\(F_A\\) kills this kernel. On the split semisimple algebra \\(A/R\\), the cocenter has one matrix-trace coordinate per simple factor, and \\(F_A\\) raises each coordinate to its \\(p\\)th power. It is bijective because \\(k\\) is perfect.</p><p>The descending images stabilize to a subspace \\(S\\). Choose \\(m\\) so that \\(S=\\operatorname{im}F_A^m\\) and \\(F_A^m\\) kills the kernel of the quotient map \\(q\\). The map \\(F_A\\) is bijective on \\(S\\). Thus \\(S\\cap\\ker q=0\\). Bijectivity downstairs also shows \\(q(S)=V(A/R)\\), so\n\\[\nS\\simeq V(A/R).\n\\]\nSince \\((Ea)^p=Ea^p\\), the assertions respect central-idempotent summands.</p><p>For a group algebra, the cocenter is free on conjugacy classes represented by individual group elements. Sufficiently high \\(p\\)-powers land on \\(p'\\)-elements, and taking \\(p\\)th powers permutes the \\(p'\\)-classes. Finally, \\(V(\\mathcal OX)\\) is free on the same class basis. The lift of \\(E\\) projects it onto a free direct summand whose rank is unchanged over \\(k\\) and \\(K\\); split semisimplicity over \\(K\\) identifies this rank with \\(k_{\\mathrm{ord}}(X,E)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.6, Subsection formula and support", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(X\\) and \\(E\\) as in Proposition 3, equation (3.5) holds, and multiplication by \\(E\\) preserves the section decompositions of the cocenter and the center.", "statement": "<p>For \\(X\\) and \\(E\\) as in Proposition 3, equation (3.5) holds, and multiplication by \\(E\\) preserves the section decompositions of the cocenter and the center.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix a \\(p\\)-element \\(u\\) and put \\(C=C_X(u)\\). The map \\(t\\mapsto ut\\) identifies the \\(p'\\)-class span of \\(V(kC)\\) with the section span belonging to \\(u\\). Conjugacy of \\(ut\\) and \\(ut'\\) forces the conjugating element to centralize their common \\(p\\)-part \\(u\\).</p><p>For \\(t\\in C\\) a \\(p'\\)-element, conjugation by \\(\\langle u\\rangle\\) in the cocenter expansion gives\n\\[\n\\overline{Eut}\n=\n\\overline{\\operatorname{Br}_{\\langle u\\rangle}(E)ut}.\n\\]\nCoefficients are constant on the orbits, and their products with \\(ut\\) have equal cocenter classes, so only fixed orbits survive. The restricted idempotent preserves the \\(p'\\)-class span by Lemma 3.1. Multiplication by \\(u\\) intertwines the idempotent actions, whose ranks are the terms of (3.5). Summing over sections proves the formula and cocenter preservation.</p><p>For the center use the nondegenerate pairing\n\\[\nZ(kX)\\times V(kX)\\longrightarrow k,\n\\qquad\n(a,\\overline b)\\longmapsto\\tau(ab),\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\tau\\) takes the coefficient of \\(1\\). A class sum pairs with an individual representative of its inverse class with coefficient one. Multiplication by \\(E\\) is self-adjoint. Center sections pair with inverse cocenter sections, giving preservation on the center without division by class sizes.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Chain cancellations; original Section 3.3.", "statement": "<p>Chain cancellations; original Section 3.3.</p><p>Use the chains \\(C\\), stabilizers \\(H_C\\), and idempotents \\(E_C\\) from Proposition 2. Then\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{C/X\\\\C\\text{ normal}}}\n(-1)^{|C|}l(H_C,E_C)\n=\n\\sum_{\\substack{C/X\\\\C\\text{ normal}}}\n(-1)^{|C|}k_{\\mathrm{ord}}(H_C,E_C).\n\\tag{3.6}\n\\]\nBoth sums may equivalently be taken over all chains.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 4.1 permits removal or insertion of nonnormal chains for both counts. Lemma 4.2 proves equality of their alternating sums.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.7, Removal of nonnormal chains", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In an alternating sum over chain orbits, every contribution depending only on \\(H_C\\) and the terminal group \\(Q_r\\) cancels on the nonnormal chains.", "statement": "<p>In an alternating sum over chain orbits, every contribution depending only on \\(H_C\\) and the terminal group \\(Q_r\\) cancels on the nonnormal chains.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a nonnormal chain, let \\(i\\) be the largest index such that \\(A=Q_i\\) is not normal in \\(P=Q_r\\), and let \\(R\\) be the normal closure of \\(A\\) in \\(P\\). Then\n\\[\nA&lt;R\\leq Q_{i+1}.\n\\]\nAlso \\(R&lt;P\\): a maximal subgroup of \\(P\\) containing the proper subgroup \\(A\\) is normal and contains \\(R\\).</p><p>Insert \\(R\\) immediately after \\(A\\) if absent, and remove it if present. The last group \\(P\\) and last nonnormal member \\(A\\) are unchanged, so this is an involution. Every element normalizing \\(A\\) and \\(P\\) normalizes \\(R\\); hence \\(H_C\\) is unchanged. The operation is \\(X\\)-equivariant and reverses the sign, so it gives cancellation on chain orbits.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.8, Alternating subsection cancellation", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Equation (3.6) holds.", "statement": "<p>Equation (3.6) holds.</p>", "proof": "<p>By Lemma 4.1 take both sums over all chains. Expand the ordinary count using Proposition 3. The \\(u=1\\) terms give the simple-module sum. The other terms are indexed by \\(X\\)-orbits of pairs \\((C,u)\\), where \\(u\\ne1\\) is a \\(p\\)-element of \\(H_C\\), and contribute\n\\[\nl\\bigl(H_C\\cap C_X(u),\\operatorname{Br}_{Q_r\\langle u\\rangle}(E)\\bigr).\n\\tag{3.7}\n\\]\nThe displayed idempotent follows because successive coefficient restrictions have support\n\\[\nC_X(Q_r)\\cap C_X(u)=C_X(Q_r\\langle u\\rangle).\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(i\\) be the largest index for which \\(u\\notin Q_i\\), allowing \\(i=0,r\\). Toggle\n\\[\nR=Q_i\\langle u\\rangle\n\\]\nimmediately after \\(Q_i\\). It is a \\(p\\)-group, is contained in every later member, and the last member not containing \\(u\\) remains \\(Q_i\\). Thus the operation is involutive, including terminal insertion or deletion.</p><p>Every element centralizing \\(u\\) and normalizing \\(Q_i\\) normalizes \\(R\\). Hence \\(H_C\\cap C_X(u)\\), and also the product of the terminal member with \\(\\langle u\\rangle\\), are unchanged. The contribution (3.7) is preserved. The operation is equivariant and sign-reversing, so all \\(u\\ne1\\) terms cancel.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Defect-zero detector; original Lemma 3.10.", "statement": "<p>Defect-zero detector; original Lemma 3.10.</p><p>Let \\(X\\), \\(k\\), and \\(E\\) be as in Proposition 2, and put\n\\[\nc_X=\\sum_{\\substack{u\\in X\\\\u\\text{ a }p\\text{-element}}}u,\n\\qquad\nv=E(1-c_X).\n\\]\nCall a block semisimple if its block algebra is semisimple, hence one full matrix algebra over \\(k\\).</p><p>The central element \\(v\\) is supported on \\(p\\)-singular elements. For a positive-length normal chain \\(C\\),\n\\[\n\\lambda_b(s_{H_C}(v))\n=\n\\lambda_b(E_C)\\in\\{0,1\\}\n\\]\nfor every block \\(b\\) of \\(H_C\\). At the length-zero chain its scalar is one on the nonsemisimple blocks selected by \\(E\\) and zero on every other block.</p>", "proof": "<p>The element \\(1-c_X\\) is the negative sum of the nonidentity \\(p\\)-elements, so belongs entirely to \\(p\\)-singular sections. Proposition 3 gives the same support for \\(v\\).</p><p>If \\(B_0\\) is a semisimple block idempotent, \\(B_0v\\) belongs to the one-dimensional center \\(kB_0\\). Section preservation makes its support \\(p\\)-singular, whereas Lemma 5.1 makes the support of \\(B_0\\) disjoint from \\(p\\)-singular elements. Thus \\(B_0v=0\\). On nonsemisimple blocks, Lemma 5.1 gives \\(\\lambda_b(v)=\\lambda_b(E)\\), proving the length-zero assertion.</p><p>For a positive-length normal chain, put \\(Q=Q_r\\ne1\\) and \\(H=H_C\\). By Lemma 2.1, \\(\\lambda_b\\circ s_H\\) is a central character of \\(X\\) factoring through \\(\\operatorname{Br}_Q\\). It cannot be the character of a semisimple block \\(B_0\\), because \\(\\operatorname{Br}_Q(B_0)=0\\). On its nonsemisimple block the values on \\(v\\) and \\(E\\) agree. Applying Lemma 2.1 to \\(E\\) gives \\(\\lambda_b(E_C)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.9, Averaging and defect zero", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a finite group \\(X\\), put \\( d_X=\\sum_{x,y\\in X}[x,y], \\qquad I_X(a)=\\sum_{x\\in X}xax^{-1}, \\qquad [x,y]=xyx^{-1}y^{-1}. \\) The image of \\(I_X\\), and \\(d_X\\), annihilate \\(\\operatorname{rad}(kX)\\).", "statement": "<p>For a finite group \\(X\\), put\n\\[\nd_X=\\sum_{x,y\\in X}[x,y],\n\\qquad\nI_X(a)=\\sum_{x\\in X}xax^{-1},\n\\qquad\n[x,y]=xyx^{-1}y^{-1}.\n\\]\nThe image of \\(I_X\\), and \\(d_X\\), annihilate \\(\\operatorname{rad}(kX)\\). The central scalar of \\(d_X\\) is nonzero on semisimple blocks and its central character is zero on other blocks.</p><p>Each semisimple block contains exactly one ordinary irreducible, of defect zero, and every ordinary defect-zero irreducible occurs this way. Furthermore:\n\\[\n\\operatorname{im}I_X\n=\n\\operatorname{span}\\{\\text{class sums of }g:p\\nmid |C_X(g)|\\};\n\\]\neach semisimple block idempotent belongs to \\(\\operatorname{im}I_X\\) and has zero Brauer image at every nontrivial \\(p\\)-subgroup; and\n\\[\n\\lambda_b(c_X)=0\n\\]\non every nonsemisimple block \\(b\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The symmetric form \\((a,b)\\mapsto\\tau(ab)\\) on \\(kX\\) is nondegenerate. For dual bases \\(v_i,v_i^*\\), the tensor \\(\\sum_i v_i\\otimes v_i^*\\) is basis-independent. Contracting it, and its tensor square, gives\n\\[\nI_X(a)=\\sum_i v_iav_i^*,\n\\qquad\nd_X=\\sum_{i,j}v_iv_jv_i^*v_j^*\n=\\sum_j I_X(v_j)v_j^*.\n\\tag{3.8}\n\\]\nFor \\(b\\in\\operatorname{rad}(kX)\\),\n\\[\n\\tau(I_X(a)b)=\\operatorname{Tr}(L_bR_a)=0,\n\\]\nbecause \\(L_b\\) is nilpotent and commutes with \\(R_a\\). Replacing \\(b\\) by \\(bc\\) and using nondegeneracy proves \\(I_X(a)b=0\\). The last expression in (3.8) gives radical annihilation by \\(d_X\\).</p><p>On a semisimple block \\(M_d(k)\\), the form is \\(t\\operatorname{Tr}\\) for some \\(t\\in k^\\times\\). Matrix units have duals \\(t^{-1}e_{ji}\\), so\n\\[\nI_X(a)=t^{-1}\\operatorname{Tr}(a)1,\n\\qquad\nd_X=t^{-2}1.\n\\tag{3.9}\n\\]\nIts block idempotent lies in the image, by taking \\(a=te_{11}\\). On a nonsemisimple block, a central element with nonzero residue scalar is a unit in the local center and cannot annihilate the nonzero radical.</p><p>For an ordinary irreducible \\(\\chi\\), Schur's lemma and character orthogonality give\n\\[\n\\lambda_\\chi(d_X)=\\frac{|X|^2}{\\chi(1)^2}.\n\\tag{3.10}\n\\]\nThis scalar is integral and reduces nontrivially exactly in defect zero. The modular calculation therefore places exactly the defect-zero characters in semisimple blocks. Such a block has cocenter dimension one; Proposition 3 shows that its lift selects exactly one ordinary irreducible.</p><p>For \\(g\\in X\\),\n\\[\nI_X(g)=|C_X(g)|\\sum_{a\\in[g]}a,\n\\]\nproving the image description. A semisimple block idempotent therefore has no support on elements centralized by a nontrivial \\(p\\)-subgroup, proving its Brauer-image assertion.</p><p>For a nontrivial \\(p\\)-subgroup \\(P\\), choose \\(z\\in Z(P)\\) of order \\(p\\). It is central in \\(C_X(P)\\), and multiplication by \\(z\\) preserves its \\(p\\)-elements. Their sum factors as\n\\[\nq\\cdot(\\text{sum of orbit representatives}),\n\\qquad\nq=\\sum_{j=0}^{p-1}z^j,\n\\qquad\nq^2=pq=0.\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Br}_P(c_X)^2=0,\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Br}_P(c_X^2)=0.\n\\]\nIf \\(p\\mid |C_X(g)|\\), choose a nontrivial \\(P\\leq C_X(g)\\); the coefficient of \\(g\\) in \\(c_X^2\\) vanishes. Thus \\(c_X^2\\in\\operatorname{im}I_X\\). On a nonsemisimple block its central character vanishes by radical annihilation. Hence\n\\[\n\\lambda_b(c_X)^2=\\lambda_b(c_X^2)=0.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 3.14, Reduction to uniform tuple divisibility", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assume the following counting assertion for every finite group \\(J\\) with \\(O_p(J)=1\\).", "statement": "<p>Assume the following counting assertion for every finite group \\(J\\) with \\(O_p(J)=1\\). For \\(n=p^s\\) and a multiset \\(M\\) of \\(n\\) conjugacy classes of \\(p\\)-singular elements of \\(J\\), let \\(N_{J,s}(M)\\) count, modulo simultaneous \\(J\\)-conjugation, the ordered tuples\n\\[\n(x,y,u_1,\\ldots,u_n)\\in J^{n+2}\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\n[x,y]u_1\\cdots u_n=1,\n\\qquad\n\\langle x,y,u_1,\\ldots,u_n\\rangle=J,\n\\qquad\n\\{[u_1],\\ldots,[u_n]\\}=M.\n\\]\nFor each \\(h\\geq1\\), assume \\(p^h\\mid N_{J,s}(M)\\) for all sufficiently large \\(s\\), uniformly in \\(M\\).</p><p>Then, for every finite group \\(X\\) and central idempotent \\(E\\in kX\\),\n\\[\nl(X,E)=((1+T)z)(X,E).\n\\]\nIn particular, the blockwise weight equality follows with the assignment of Theorem 1.</p>", "proof": "<p>A chain orbit has size \\(|X|/|H_C|\\). Lemma 6.2 therefore converts (3.11) into\n\\[\n\\Phi_n\n=\n\\frac1{|X|}\n\\sum_{\\substack{x,y,u_1,\\ldots,u_n\\in X\\\\{}\n[x,y]u_1\\cdots u_n=1}}\na_X(\\langle x,y,u_1,\\ldots,u_n\\rangle)\n\\prod_{i=1}^n\\alpha_{u_i}.\n\\tag{3.15}\n\\]\nFor a fixed tuple, the admissible actual chains are exactly those normalized by its generated subgroup.</p><p>Lemma 6.3 removes generated subgroups with nontrivial \\(p\\)-core. The support of \\(\\widehat v\\) removes tuples having a nonsingular puncture entry. Fix a remaining subgroup \\(J\\). Group its generating tuples by the multiset \\(M\\) of \\(J\\)-classes of the \\(u_i\\). The coefficient product is constant on \\(M\\); call it \\(\\alpha(M)\\). A generating tuple has simultaneous-conjugation stabilizer \\(Z(J)\\), so the numerator in (3.15), for \\(n=p^s\\), is\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{J\\leq X\\\\O_p(J)=1}}\na_X(J)\\frac{|J|}{|Z(J)|}\n\\sum_M\\alpha(M)N_{J,s}(M).\n\\tag{3.16}\n\\]</p><p>For each \\(h\\), uniform divisibility puts every inner summand in \\(p^h\\mathcal O\\) for sufficiently large \\(s\\). The sum is finite for each \\(s\\), and there are finitely many subgroups \\(J\\), so its numerator tends to zero. The fixed denominator \\(|X|\\) does not change this conclusion.</p><p>By Lemma 6.1 and Proposition 4, the resulting equality is (3.4). It is an equality of integers because it holds in characteristic zero. Proposition 2 now gives \\(l=(1+T)z\\), with the required weight interpretation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.11, The first trace limit", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Choose a class-sum lift \\( \\widehat v=\\sum_{a\\in X}\\alpha_a a\\in Z(\\mathcal OX) \\) of \\(v=E(1-c_X)\\), with \\(\\alpha_a=0\\) unless \\(a\\) is \\(p\\)-singular.", "statement": "<p>Choose a class-sum lift\n\\[\n\\widehat v=\\sum_{a\\in X}\\alpha_a a\\in Z(\\mathcal OX)\n\\]\nof \\(v=E(1-c_X)\\), with \\(\\alpha_a=0\\) unless \\(a\\) is \\(p\\)-singular. For \\(n\\geq1\\), put\n\\[\n\\Phi_n\n=\n\\sum_{C/X}(-1)^{|C|}\n\\operatorname{Tr}\\bigl(s_{H_C}(\\widehat v)^n\\mid V(KH_C)\\bigr),\n\\tag{3.11}\n\\]\nsumming over all chains. Then\n\\[\n\\lim_{s\\to\\infty}\\Phi_{p^s}\n=\n\\sum_{\\substack{C/X\\\\C\\text{ normal}}}\n(-1)^{|C|}k_{\\mathrm{ord}}(H_C,E_C)\n-\nz(X,E).\n\\tag{3.12}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Nonnormal chains cancel by Lemma 4.1, since their traces depend only on the stabilizer. For a normal chain, \\(V(KH_C)\\) has one coordinate per ordinary irreducible. The eigenvalues are integral central scalars reducing to those in Proposition 5.</p><p>An eigenvalue reducing to zero has \\(p^s\\)th powers tending to zero. If it is \\(1+a\\), with \\(\\operatorname{val}(a)&gt;0\\), then\n\\[\n\\operatorname{val}((1+a)^p-1)\n\\geq\n\\min\\{\\operatorname{val}(p)+\\operatorname{val}(a),\np\\operatorname{val}(a)\\}.\n\\]\nAfter normalizing the discrete valuation to integer values, each iteration increases its positive value by at least one unless the difference is already zero. The powers tend to one, also for \\(p=2\\).</p><p>For positive-length chains the limit counts all selected ordinary irreducibles. At length zero it omits the semisimple blocks, each contributing one defect-zero character by Lemma 5.1. There are finitely many terms, giving (3.12).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.12, Trace normalization", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(H\\leq X\\), let \\(w=s_H(\\widehat v)\\).", "statement": "<p>For \\(H\\leq X\\), let \\(w=s_H(\\widehat v)\\). Then\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}(w^n\\mid V(KH))\n=\n\\frac1{|H|}\n\\sum_{\\substack{x,y,u_1,\\ldots,u_n\\in H\\\\{}\n[x,y]u_1\\cdots u_n=1}}\n\\prod_{i=1}^n\\alpha_{u_i}.\n\\tag{3.13}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The numerator is \\(\\tau(d_Hw^n)\\). The regular trace of an element of \\(KH\\) is \\(|H|\\) times its identity coefficient. Using (3.10) and the regular decomposition,\n\\[\n|H|\\tau(d_Hw^n)\n=\n\\sum_{\\chi\\in\\operatorname{Irr}(H)}\n\\chi(1)^2\\frac{|H|^2}{\\chi(1)^2}\\lambda_\\chi(w)^n\n=\n|H|^2\\operatorname{Tr}(w^n\\mid V(KH)).\n\\]\nDivision gives the single denominator \\(|H|\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.3", "orig": "Lemma 3.13, Cancellation by a normal \\(p\\)-subgroup", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(J\\leq X\\), define a sum over actual chains, without quotienting by conjugation:", "statement": "<p>For \\(J\\leq X\\), define a sum over actual chains, without quotienting by conjugation:\n\\[\na_X(J)=\\sum_{\\substack{C\\text{ a chain in }X\\\\J\\leq H_C}}(-1)^{|C|}.\n\\tag{3.14}\n\\]\nIf \\(O_p(J)\\ne1\\), then \\(a_X(J)=0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(P=O_p(J)\\). In a chain normalized by \\(J\\), every \\(Q_iP\\) is a \\(p\\)-subgroup normalized by \\(J\\). Let \\(i\\) be the last index with \\(P\\nleq Q_i\\); it exists because \\(Q_0=1\\) and \\(P\\ne1\\). Toggle \\(Q_iP\\) immediately after \\(Q_i\\). It strictly contains \\(Q_i\\), lies in every later member, and preserves the last member not containing \\(P\\). This is a sign-reversing involution, including the length-zero chain and terminal insertion or deletion, on the chains normalized by \\(J\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Proposition 4.4, Translated cyclic twist and index", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fix a prime \\(p\\), put \\(\\kappa=\\overline{\\mathbb F}_p\\), let \\(s\\geq1\\), and put \\(n=p^s\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix a prime \\(p\\), put \\(\\kappa=\\overline{\\mathbb F}_p\\), let \\(s\\geq1\\), and put \\(n=p^s\\). There are an elliptic curve \\(A_0/\\kappa\\), a point \\(P\\in A_0(\\kappa)\\) of exact order \\(n\\), finitely generated fields \\(F_0\\subset F'\\) of transcendence degree \\(n\\) over \\(\\kappa\\), and a smooth projective geometrically integral genus-one curve \\(E/F_0\\) such that:</p><p>\\[\nF'=\\kappa(A_0^n),\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Gal}(F'/F_0)=\\langle\\sigma\\rangle,\n\\qquad\n[F':F_0]=n,\n\\]\nand, for the generic projections \\(t_i\\),\n\\[\n\\sigma(t_i)=t_{i+1}-P.\n\\tag{4.6}\n\\]</p><p>Under \\(E_{F'}\\simeq A_{0,F'}\\), descent acts by \\(x\\mapsto\\sigma(x)+P\\). The disjoint divisors\n\\[\nB_{F'}=\\sum_{i=1}^n[t_i],\n\\qquad\nD_{F'}=\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}[jP]\n\\tag{4.7}\n\\]\ndescend to finite étale effective divisors \\(B,D\\) of degree \\(n\\), with \\(D\\) ample.</p><p>The index of \\(E/F_0\\), namely the positive generator of its divisor degrees, is exactly \\(n\\). For every nonzero invariant differential \\(\\eta\\) on \\(A_0\\),\n\\[\n\\Omega_{F'/\\kappa}\n=\n\\bigoplus_{i=1}^nF't_i^*\\eta.\n\\tag{4.10}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(A_0,P\\) by Lemma 7.1. The automorphism in (4.6) satisfies\n\\[\n\\sigma^a(t_i)=t_{i+a}-[a]P,\n\\qquad\n\\sigma^n=1.\n\\]\nFor \\(0&lt;a&lt;n\\), independent projections cannot differ by a constant, so \\(\\sigma^a\\ne1\\). Fixed-field theory gives the cyclic extension of degree \\(n\\). The intermediate field \\(F_0\\) is finitely generated of transcendence degree \\(n\\).</p><p>The semilinear action\n\\[\n\\delta(x)=\\sigma(x)+P\n\\]\nhas order \\(n\\), sends \\(t_i\\) to \\(t_{i+1}\\), and sends \\(jP\\) to \\((j+1)P\\). The two divisors in (4.7) are invariant and have disjoint support. The invariant divisor \\(D_{F'}\\) supplies a canonical linearization of \\(\\mathcal O(D_{F'})\\). Its third power is a tensor product of translates of the ample Weierstrass bundle \\(\\mathcal O(3[0])\\). Projective Galois descent gives \\(E,B,D\\). Smoothness, geometric integrality, finiteness, étaleness, and ampleness descend. Cohomology base change gives genus one.</p><p>Let a divisor on \\(E\\) have degree \\(b\\). Pull it to \\(A_{0,F'}\\) and let \\(v\\in A_0(F')\\) be its group-law sum, using Lemma 7.2. Invariance gives\n\\[\n\\sigma(v)+[b]P=v.\n\\tag{4.8}\n\\]\nBy Lemma 7.3,\n\\[\nv=c+\\sum_{i=1}^n\\beta_i(t_i).\n\\]\nSubstitution yields\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^n(\\beta_{i-1}-\\beta_i)(t_i)\n+[b]P-\\sum_{i=1}^n\\beta_i(P)=0,\n\\qquad\n\\beta_0=\\beta_n.\n\\tag{4.9}\n\\]\nEvaluate first at the origin and then along each factor. All \\(\\beta_i\\) equal one endomorphism \\(\\beta\\), and\n\\[\n\\sum_i\\beta_i(P)=\\beta([n]P)=0.\n\\]\nThus \\([b]P=0\\), so \\(n\\mid b\\). Since \\(D\\) has degree \\(n\\), the index is exactly \\(n\\).</p><p>Finally, the product formula for differentials at the generic point of \\(A_0^n\\) gives (4.10). These are absolute field differentials, not relative differentials on the elliptic curve.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every prime \\(p\\), there is an elliptic curve \\(A_0/\\kappa\\), where \\(\\kappa=\\overline{\\mathbb F}_p\\), with a point of order \\(p\\).", "statement": "<p>For every prime \\(p\\), there is an elliptic curve \\(A_0/\\kappa\\), where \\(\\kappa=\\overline{\\mathbb F}_p\\), with a point of order \\(p\\). On this same curve, for every \\(s\\geq1\\), there is a point of exact order \\(p^s\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(p=2\\), take\n\\[\ny^2+xy=x^3+1.\n\\]\nIts affine partial derivatives could vanish simultaneously only at \\((0,0)\\), which is not on the curve. Its projective origin is smooth. Its \\(\\mathbb F_2\\)-points are\n\\[\n[0:1:0],\\quad(0,1),\\quad(1,0),\\quad(1,1),\n\\]\nso Sylow theory supplies a point of order \\(2\\).</p><p>For odd \\(p\\), put \\(r=(p-1)/2\\) and\n\\[\nf_\\lambda(x)=x(x-1)(x-\\lambda),\n\\qquad\nH(\\lambda)=[x^{p-1}]f_\\lambda(x)^r.\n\\]\nThe coefficient of \\(\\lambda^r\\) in \\(H\\) is \\((-1)^r\\), so choose \\(\\lambda\\notin\\{0,1\\}\\) with \\(H(\\lambda)\\ne0\\). Then\n\\[\nA_0:y^2=f_\\lambda(x)\n\\]\nis smooth.</p><p>Choose \\(q=p^\\ell\\) with \\(\\lambda\\in\\mathbb F_q\\). Reducing the quadratic-character point count modulo \\(p\\) gives\n\\[\n\\#A_0(\\mathbb F_q)\n\\equiv\n1+\\sum_{x\\in\\mathbb F_q}f_\\lambda(x)^{(q-1)/2}\n=\n1-H(\\lambda)^{1+p+\\cdots+p^{\\ell-1}}.\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\nIndeed, the relevant exponents range from \\((q-1)/2\\) to \\(3(q-1)/2\\), and only \\(q-1\\) is a positive multiple of \\(q-1\\). The sum of \\(x^{q-1}\\) is \\(-1\\), and the other sums vanish.</p><p>For the coefficient calculation, use\n\\[\nf_\\lambda(x)^{(q-1)/2}\n=\n\\prod_{j=0}^{\\ell-1}(f_\\lambda(x)^r)^{p^j}.\n\\]\nIf\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=0}^{\\ell-1}p^ja_j=p^\\ell-1,\n\\qquad\na_j\\in[r,3r],\n\\]\nreduction modulo \\(p\\) forces \\(a_0=p-1\\); subtracting and dividing by \\(p\\) repeats the argument. Thus every \\(a_j=p-1\\), proving (4.1).</p><p>The nonzero element\n\\[\nc=H(\\lambda)^{1+p+\\cdots+p^{\\ell-1}}\n\\]\nlies in \\(\\mathbb F_p^\\times\\). Passing to \\(\\mathbb F_{q^d}\\), with \\(d\\) a multiple of its order, makes the corresponding expression \\(c^d=1\\). Hence \\(p\\mid\\#A_0(\\mathbb F_{q^d})\\), and Sylow theory gives a point of order \\(p\\).</p><p>Multiplication by \\(p\\) is finite locally free of degree \\(p^2\\), hence surjective on \\(\\kappa\\)-points. If \\(Q\\) has order \\(p^a\\) and \\([p]Q'=Q\\), then \\(Q'\\) has order \\(p^{a+1}\\). Repeated preimages give all orders \\(p^s\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(A/\\kappa\\) be an elliptic curve and \\(U/\\kappa\\) a field extension.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(A/\\kappa\\) be an elliptic curve and \\(U/\\kappa\\) a field extension. For every zero-cycle on \\(A_U\\), its group-law sum over an algebraic closure of \\(U\\), with full geometric multiplicities, belongs to \\(A(U)\\). The sum is additive for signed cycles. Translating a cycle of degree \\(b\\) by \\(Q\\in A(U)\\) adds \\([b]Q\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(K_A=\\kappa(A)\\). A nonzero invariant differential generates \\(\\Omega_{K_A/\\kappa}\\), and its pullback by \\([p]\\) is zero. The kernel of\n\\[\nd:K_A\\to\\Omega_{K_A/\\kappa}\n\\]\nis \\(K_A^p\\): it is a proper subfield containing \\(K_A^p\\), and \\([K_A:K_A^p]=p\\). Therefore\n\\[\n[p]^*K_A\\subseteq K_A^p.\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\nFor every \\(V/\\kappa\\), this gives\n\\[\n[p](A(V))\\subseteq A(V^p).\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]\nTo justify evaluation, if \\(f\\) is regular on an affine open containing \\([p]R\\), write \\([p]^*f=g^p\\). The function \\(g\\) is regular on its inverse image because its \\(p\\)th power is regular and the smooth curve is normal. Thus \\(f([p]R)=g(R)^p\\). Iteration gives\n\\[\n[p^u](A(V))\\subseteq A(V^{p^u}).\n\\]</p><p>For a closed point with residue \\(T/U\\), let \\(T_{\\mathrm{sep}}\\) be its maximal separable subextension and write \\([T:T_{\\mathrm{sep}}]=p^u\\). Then \\(T^{p^u}\\subseteq T_{\\mathrm{sep}}\\). Its geometric cycle has points \\(R_\\tau\\), one for each embedding of \\(T_{\\mathrm{sep}}\\), each with multiplicity \\(p^u\\). After splitting the separable extension, the remaining tensor factor is local of dimension \\(p^u\\), proving that multiplicity. Each embedding extends uniquely to \\(T\\), and\n\\[\n[p^u]R_\\tau\\in A(\\tau(T_{\\mathrm{sep}})).\n\\]\nTheir sum belongs to \\(A(U^{\\mathrm{sep}})\\) and is Galois-invariant, hence belongs to \\(A(U)\\). Additivity and the translation formula follow from the geometric sum.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.3", "orig": "Lemma 4.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(A/\\kappa\\) be an elliptic curve.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(A/\\kappa\\) be an elliptic curve.</p><p>For every extension \\(V/\\kappa\\), an origin-preserving morphism \\(A_V\\to A_V\\) is a group endomorphism defined over \\(\\kappa\\).</p><p>Every rational map \\(A^n\\dashrightarrow A\\) has a unique expression\n\\[\n(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)\\longmapsto\nc+\\sum_{i=1}^n\\beta_i(x_i),\n\\qquad\nc\\in A(\\kappa),\\quad\n\\beta_i\\in\\operatorname{End}_\\kappa(A).\n\\tag{4.4}\n\\]\nIn particular, it extends over the whole product.</p>", "proof": "<p>After extending \\(V\\) algebraically, let \\(f(0)=0\\) and form\n\\[\ng(x,y)=f(x+y)-f(x)-f(y).\n\\]\nFor \\(a\\ne0\\) on the target, the projection of \\(g^{-1}(a)\\) to the second factor is closed by properness and omits \\(0\\). On its nonempty open complement \\(W\\), the map \\(x\\mapsto g(x,y)\\) misses \\(a\\), so is constant; its value at \\(0\\) is zero. Hence \\(g=0\\) on \\(A\\times W\\), and therefore everywhere.</p><p>Every point of \\(A(\\kappa)\\) is defined over a finite field and is torsion. Its image under a homomorphism is torsion. Since each finite scheme \\(A[N]\\) has only \\(\\kappa\\)-points with values in extension fields,\n\\[\nf(A(\\kappa))\\subseteq A(\\kappa).\n\\tag{4.5}\n\\]\nFor \\(h\\in\\kappa(A)\\), write\n\\[\nf^*h=\\frac ab,\n\\qquad\na=\\sum_{j=1}^tc_ja_j,\\quad\nb=\\sum_{j=1}^tc_jb_j,\n\\]\nwith \\(c_j\\in V\\) linearly independent over \\(\\kappa\\) and \\(a_j,b_j\\in\\kappa(A)\\). Choose \\(j_0\\) with \\(b_{j_0}\\ne0\\). For all but finitely many \\(R\\in A(\\kappa)\\), evaluation is defined and \\(f^*h(R)=\\lambda_R\\in\\kappa\\). Independence gives\n\\[\na_j(R)=\\lambda_Rb_j(R),\n\\qquad\nf^*h(R)=a_{j_0}(R)/b_{j_0}(R).\n\\]\nInfinitely many such evaluations force\n\\[\nf^*h=a_{j_0}/b_{j_0}\\in\\kappa(A).\n\\]\nThus \\(f\\) is defined rationally over \\(\\kappa\\), and properness extends it over the smooth source curve.</p><p>For the product assertion, view the rational map in the first variable over \\(V=\\kappa(A^{n-1})\\). Properness extends it to \\(A_V\\to A_V\\). Subtracting its value at the origin leaves an endomorphism \\(\\beta_1\\) defined over \\(\\kappa\\). Repeat with the remaining factors. The resulting expression is everywhere a morphism; its value at the origin and its restrictions to the factors give uniqueness.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Finite-extension properties of the marked twist; original Section 4.4.", "statement": "<p>Finite-extension properties of the marked twist; original Section 4.4.</p><p>Use the data of Proposition 7. Let \\(h\\geq1\\), \\(s\\geq h\\), and let \\(F/F_0\\) be finite with \\([F:F_0]_p\\leq p^h\\). Put \\(N=p^{s-h}\\), and choose \\(S=FF'\\).</p><p>Every divisor degree on \\(E_F\\), and on every regular projective integral curve finite over \\(E_F\\), is divisible by \\(N\\). Over \\(S\\), every subset of the marked absolute differentials, and of the marked normal covectors, loses at most \\(h\\) dimensions of independence.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 8.1 gives the divisor-index property, and Lemma 8.2 gives the differential-rank property. On the constant curve \\(A_{0,S}\\), absolute cotangents split into the invariant-differential line and constant differentials. Restriction along \\(t_i\\) is\n\\[\nS\\oplus\\Omega_{S/\\kappa}\\longrightarrow\\Omega_{S/\\kappa},\n\\qquad\n(\\lambda,\\beta)\\longmapsto\\lambda t_i^*\\eta+\\beta,\n\\]\nwhose kernel is generated by \\((1,-t_i^*\\eta)\\). These are the normal covectors used in the genus estimate.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "Corollary 4.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under Proposition 8's assumptions, every divisor on \\(E_F\\) has degree over \\(F\\) divisible by \\(p^{s-h}\\).", "statement": "<p>Under Proposition 8's assumptions, every divisor on \\(E_F\\) has degree over \\(F\\) divisible by \\(p^{s-h}\\). If \\(C/F\\) is regular, projective, and integral with a finite dominant map \\(C\\to E_F\\), every divisor and invertible sheaf on \\(C\\) has degree over \\(F\\) divisible by \\(p^{s-h}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(a=[F:F_0]\\). A closed point \\(z\\) of \\(E_F\\), mapping to \\(x\\in E\\), pushes forward to \\([\\kappa(z):\\kappa(x)]x\\), whose degree over \\(F_0\\) is\n\\[\n[\\kappa(z):F_0]=a[\\kappa(z):F].\n\\]\nThus a divisor of degree \\(b\\) over \\(F\\) gives a divisor of degree \\(ab\\) over \\(F_0\\). Proposition 7 gives \\(p^s\\mid ab\\), and \\(a_p\\leq p^h\\) gives \\(p^{s-h}\\mid b\\).</p><p>Pushforward under \\(C\\to E_F\\) preserves degree over \\(F\\) by the same residue-field tower calculation. Every invertible sheaf on a regular integral curve is represented by the divisor of a nonzero rational section.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.6", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under Proposition 8's assumptions, \\(S/F\\) is finite Galois of degree dividing \\(n\\), and \\( [S:F']_p\\leq p^h. \\) For every subset \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}\\), the forms \\(t_i^*\\eta\\), \\(i\\in I\\), have rank at least …", "statement": "<p>Under Proposition 8's assumptions, \\(S/F\\) is finite Galois of degree dividing \\(n\\), and\n\\[\n[S:F']_p\\leq p^h.\n\\tag{4.11}\n\\]\nFor every subset \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}\\), the forms \\(t_i^*\\eta\\), \\(i\\in I\\), have rank at least \\(|I|-h\\) in \\(\\Omega_{S/\\kappa}\\). The covectors\n\\[\n(1,-t_i^*\\eta)\\in S\\oplus\\Omega_{S/\\kappa}\n\\tag{4.12}\n\\]\nalso have rank at least \\(|I|-h\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Since \\(F'/F_0\\) is Galois, \\(S/F\\) is Galois with group a subgroup of its cyclic group. The tower identity\n\\[\n[S:F'][F':F_0]=[S:F][F:F_0]\n\\]\ngives (4.11).</p><p>The first two spaces in\n\\[\nS\\otimes_{F'}\\Omega_{F'/\\kappa}\n\\longrightarrow\n\\Omega_{S/\\kappa}\n\\longrightarrow\n\\Omega_{S/F'}\n\\longrightarrow0\n\\]\nboth have dimension \\(n\\). Thus the kernel of the first map has dimension \\(\\dim_S\\Omega_{S/F'}\\). If \\(S_{\\mathrm{sep}}/F'\\) is the maximal separable subextension and \\([S:S_{\\mathrm{sep}}]=p^u\\), then \\(u\\leq h\\). The purely inseparable extension can be generated by at most \\(u\\) elements, whose differentials generate the relative differentials. Hence the kernel has dimension at most \\(h\\).</p><p>The original forms are a basis by Proposition 7, so any \\(|I|\\) of them lose at most \\(h\\) dimensions. Projection of the covectors to the second summand proves their rank bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Divisor degrees, the absolute determinant, and separable ramification; original Section 5.1.", "statement": "<p>Divisor degrees, the absolute determinant, and separable ramification; original Section 5.1.</p><p>For this proposition, replace the specific field \\(\\overline{\\mathbb F}_p\\) by any perfect field \\(\\kappa\\) of characteristic \\(p\\). Let \\(S/\\kappa\\) be finitely generated of transcendence degree \\(\\rho\\), and let all curves be regular integral projective curves over \\(S\\), not necessarily smooth or geometrically reduced.</p><p>The absolute cotangent determinant computes\n\\[\n\\nu(C)=-2\\chi(C)=\\deg_S\\det\\Omega_{C/\\kappa}.\n\\]\nFor a finite generically separable map, the difference of these determinant degrees is an effective ramification divisor, with local coefficient at least\n\\[\na_y-1+\\mathbf1_{p\\mid a_y}.\n\\]\nDegrees and Euler characteristics retain the full residue degrees and are unchanged numerically by finite separable scalar extension.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 9.1 supplies the degree and base-change rules. Lemma 9.2 identifies the determinant degree with \\(\\nu\\), and Lemma 9.3 supplies the effective separable ramification divisor and its wild contribution.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.2, Degrees over the stated ground field", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under Proposition 9's assumptions, for an invertible sheaf \\(\\mathcal L\\) on \\(C\\), \\( \\chi(C,\\mathcal L)=\\deg_S\\mathcal L+\\chi(C). \\) A finite dominant map \\(f:C\\to C'\\) of function-field degree \\(\\delta\\) is finite …", "statement": "<p>Under Proposition 9's assumptions, for an invertible sheaf \\(\\mathcal L\\) on \\(C\\),\n\\[\n\\chi(C,\\mathcal L)=\\deg_S\\mathcal L+\\chi(C).\n\\tag{5.3}\n\\]\nA finite dominant map \\(f:C\\to C'\\) of function-field degree \\(\\delta\\) is finite flat, and\n\\[\n\\deg_S f^*D=\\delta\\deg_S D,\n\\qquad\n\\deg_S f_*D'=\\deg_S D'.\n\\]\nThese assertions and Euler characteristics add on disjoint unions. Finite separable extension \\(S_1/S\\) preserves their numerical values over the respective ground fields.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a closed point \\(x\\), the quotient in\n\\[\n0\\to\\mathcal O_C(D)\\to\\mathcal O_C(D+x)\\to Q_x\\to0\n\\]\nhas length one over the local DVR, hence \\(S\\)-dimension \\([\\kappa(x):S]\\). Finite-support sheaves have no higher cohomology. Adding and subtracting points proves (5.3); a rational trivialization represents any line bundle by a divisor. Principal divisors consequently have degree zero.</p><p>Locally on \\(C'\\), \\(f_*\\mathcal O_C\\) is finite torsion-free over a DVR and hence free of rank \\(\\delta\\). Reduction modulo a uniformizer gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{y\\mapsto x}e_y[\\kappa(y):\\kappa(x)]=\\delta.\n\\tag{5.4}\n\\]\nMultiplying by \\([\\kappa(x):S]\\) proves the pullback formula. The pushforward formula is the residue-field tower identity.</p><p>Finite separable extension preserves regularity; the extended curve is a disjoint union of integral components. Flat cohomology base change gives\n\\[\nH^i(C_{S_1},\\mathcal G_{S_1})\n=\nH^i(C,\\mathcal G)\\otimes_S S_1.\n\\]\nDimensions, Euler characteristics, and then degrees by (5.3), have the same numerical values. Reduced finite divisors remain reduced.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "Proposition 5.3, The absolute determinant computes arithmetic genus", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Under Proposition 9's assumptions, \\(\\Omega_{C/\\kappa}\\) is locally free of rank \\(\\rho+1\\), and \\( \\deg_S\\det\\Omega_{C/\\kappa}=\\nu(C). \\)", "statement": "<p>Under Proposition 9's assumptions, \\(\\Omega_{C/\\kappa}\\) is locally free of rank \\(\\rho+1\\), and\n\\[\n\\deg_S\\det\\Omega_{C/\\kappa}=\\nu(C).\n\\tag{5.5}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Local rings of \\(C\\) are essentially of finite type over \\(\\kappa\\): choose a finitely generated domain with fraction field \\(S\\), clear coefficients in a presentation, and contract the defining prime before localization. Regularity over the perfect field makes them smooth local rings of a \\(\\kappa\\)-model. Their absolute differentials are free of rank\n\\[\n\\operatorname{trdeg}_\\kappa\\kappa(C)=\\rho+1.\n\\]\nThe cotangent sequence also gives coherence.</p><p>Choose \\(i:C\\hookrightarrow\\mathbb P_S^b\\), with ideal \\(\\mathcal I\\). This is a regular immersion of codimension \\(b-1\\). Locally, if \\(R/\\mathcal I\\) and \\(R\\) are regular, choose \\(b-1\\) elements of \\(\\mathcal I\\) giving a basis of the kernel on cotangent spaces. They form part of a regular system of parameters and cut out a regular quotient of the required dimension. The remaining prime ideal is zero, since a nonzero prime in a finite-dimensional local domain lowers dimension.</p><p>The conormal sequence is short exact:\n\\[\n0\\to\\mathcal I/\\mathcal I^2\n\\to i^*\\Omega_{\\mathbb P_S^b/\\kappa}\n\\to\\Omega_{C/\\kappa}\\to0.\n\\tag{5.6}\n\\]\nIndeed, the kernel at the right is locally free of rank \\(b-1\\), and the conormal sequence surjects onto it from a free module of that rank.</p><p>Projective-space absolute cotangents split into relative cotangents and the constant bundle of \\(\\Omega_{S/\\kappa}\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\det\\Omega_{C/\\kappa}\n\\simeq\n\\mathcal L_c\\otimes_S\\det\\Omega_{S/\\kappa},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal L_c\n=\n\\mathcal O_C(-b-1)\\otimes\\det(\\mathcal I/\\mathcal I^2)^\\vee.\n\\]\nThe constant factor has degree zero.</p><p>The Koszul resolution gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal E xt^v_{\\mathbb P_S^b}\n(i_*\\mathcal O_C,\\mathcal O(-b-1))\n=\n\\begin{cases}\ni_*\\mathcal L_c,&amp;v=b-1,\\\\\n0,&amp;v\\ne b-1.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nChanges of local equations glue the top dual Koszul term by the dual determinant of \\(\\mathcal I/\\mathcal I^2\\). The local-to-global Ext spectral sequence has one row. Projective-space duality therefore gives\n\\[\nH^r(C,\\mathcal O_C)^\\vee\n\\simeq\nH^{1-r}(C,\\mathcal L_c),\n\\qquad r=0,1.\n\\]\nHence \\(\\chi(C,\\mathcal L_c)=-\\chi(C)\\), and Lemma 9.1 gives \\(\\deg_S\\mathcal L_c=-2\\chi(C)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.3", "orig": "Lemma 5.4, A separable determinant bound, including the wild term", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(f:C\\to C'\\) satisfy Proposition 9's assumptions and be generically separable of degree \\(\\delta\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(f:C\\to C'\\) satisfy Proposition 9's assumptions and be generically separable of degree \\(\\delta\\). There is an effective divisor \\(R_f\\) such that\n\\[\n\\nu(C)=\\delta\\nu(C')+\\deg_S R_f.\n\\]\nAt a point \\(y\\) with ramification index \\(a_y\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{ord}_yR_f\n\\geq\na_y-1+\\mathbf1_{p\\mid a_y}.\n\\tag{5.7}\n\\]\nNo separability of the residue extension is required. The assertions add componentwise after finite separable extension.</p>", "proof": "<p>The map\n\\[\nf^*\\Omega_{C'/\\kappa}\\longrightarrow\\Omega_{C/\\kappa}\n\\]\nis generically an isomorphism, since the function-field extension is finite separable. Its nonzero determinant defines an effective divisor \\(R_f\\). Lemmas 9.1 and 9.2 give the degree formula.</p><p>For \\(x=f(y)\\), a uniformizer \\(t\\) of \\(\\mathcal O_{C',x}\\) has primitive absolute differential. In the conormal sequence of its residue field, the middle term has dimension \\(\\rho+1\\), the final term dimension \\(\\rho\\), so \\(dt\\) is nonzero modulo the maximal ideal and extends to a basis.</p><p>Writing \\(t=u\\tau^{a_y}\\) upstairs,\n\\[\ndt=\\tau^{a_y}du+a_yu\\tau^{a_y-1}d\\tau.\n\\]\nEvery coordinate in this column is divisible by \\(\\tau^{a_y-1}\\); if \\(p\\mid a_y\\), every coordinate is divisible by \\(\\tau^{a_y}\\). Expanding the determinant proves (5.7). Absolute differentials account for inseparable residue extensions without alteration.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Lemma 5.5, Height-one kernels and cokernels", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Use Proposition 9's setting.", "statement": "<p>Use Proposition 9's setting. Let \\(\\pi:C\\to C'\\) be finite dominant with\n\\[\n\\kappa(C)^p\\subseteq\\kappa(C'),\n\\qquad\nq=[\\kappa(C):\\kappa(C')]=p^a.\n\\]\nPut\n\\[\nT_C=(\\Omega_{C/\\kappa})^\\vee,\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal F=\\ker(T_C\\to\\pi^*T_{C'}),\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal Q=\\operatorname{coker}(T_C\\to\\pi^*T_{C'}).\n\\]\nThen \\(\\mathcal F,\\mathcal Q\\) are locally free of rank \\(a\\),\n\\[\n\\det\\mathcal Q\\simeq(\\det\\mathcal F)^{\\otimes p},\n\\tag{5.8}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\nu(C)=q\\nu(C')+(p-1)\\deg_S\\mathcal F.\n\\tag{5.9}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>On an affine open downstairs, every upstairs section has its \\(p\\)th power downstairs, by integrality and normality. This also gives a unique prime upstairs over a downstairs prime. At a closed point write \\(B\\subset A\\) for the two DVRs. Then \\(A\\) is finite free of rank \\(q\\) over \\(B\\), with \\(A^p\\subset B\\).</p><p>Let \\(\\tau\\) be an upstairs uniformizer, \\(\\epsilon\\) the ramification index, and \\(f\\) the residue degree. Since \\(\\tau^p\\in B\\), \\(\\epsilon\\mid p\\); thus \\(\\epsilon=1\\) or \\(p\\), and \\(q=\\epsilon f\\). The residue extension has exponent at most one. Choose residue generators whose degree-\\(p\\) successive adjunctions give a monomial basis, and lift them to \\(b_1,\\ldots,b_r\\in A\\). If \\(\\epsilon=p\\), append \\(\\tau\\); otherwise append nothing. This gives \\(a\\) elements with \\(c_\\ell=b_\\ell^p\\in B\\).</p><p>Filtering \\(A/tA\\) by powers of \\(\\tau\\), where \\(t\\) is a downstairs uniformizer, shows that the \\(q\\) monomials\n\\[\nb_1^{i_1}\\cdots b_a^{i_a},\n\\qquad 0\\leq i_\\ell&lt;p,\n\\]\nform a residue basis. Nakayama and freeness make them a \\(B\\)-basis. Hence\n\\[\nA\\simeq\nB[X_1,\\ldots,X_a]/(X_1^p-c_1,\\ldots,X_a^p-c_a).\n\\tag{5.10}\n\\]\nThese presentations hold on neighborhoods after shrinking so that the sections, coefficients, and basis determinant are defined.</p><p>Set\n\\[\nN=A\\otimes_B\\Omega_{B/\\kappa},\n\\qquad\nW=\\sum_\\ell A\\,dc_\\ell.\n\\]\nDifferentiating gives\n\\[\n\\Omega_{A/\\kappa}\n\\simeq\n(N/W)\\oplus\\bigoplus_{\\ell=1}^aA\\,db_\\ell.\n\\tag{5.11}\n\\]\nBoth absolute cotangent modules have rank \\(\\rho+1\\), by Proposition 9. Thus \\(N/W\\) is free of rank \\(\\rho+1-a\\), and \\(W\\) is free of rank \\(a\\), with basis \\(dc_\\ell\\).</p><p>The tangent kernel has coordinates \\(D\\mapsto(D(b_\\ell))_\\ell\\), for derivations killing \\(B\\). The cokernel is \\(W^\\vee\\), with coordinates\n\\[\n[\\delta]\\longmapsto(\\delta(c_\\ell))_\\ell.\n\\]\nA derivation of \\(B\\) extends precisely when it kills the \\(c_\\ell\\), with arbitrary values on the \\(b_\\ell\\).</p><p>On overlaps write \\(b_k'=P_k(b_1,\\ldots,b_a)\\) with coefficients in \\(B\\), and let\n\\[\nJ_{k\\ell}=\\frac{\\partial P_k}{\\partial X_\\ell}(b_1,\\ldots,b_a).\n\\]\nKernel coordinates transform by \\(J\\). Taking \\(p\\)th powers and differentiating gives\n\\[\n\\delta(c_k')=\\sum_\\ell J_{k\\ell}^p\\delta(c_\\ell),\n\\]\nso cokernel coordinates transform by \\(J^{[p]}\\). Their determinant transition is \\((\\det J)^p\\), proving (5.8).</p><p>Finally the exact sequence\n\\[\n0\\to\\mathcal F\\to T_C\\to\\pi^*T_{C'}\\to\\mathcal Q\\to0\n\\]\ngives\n\\[\n\\deg T_C=q\\deg T_{C'}-(p-1)\\deg\\mathcal F.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\deg T_C=-\\nu(C)\\), equation (5.9) follows.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Bounded-error inseparable genus estimate; the differential part of the proof of original Theorem 5.1.", "statement": "<p>Bounded-error inseparable genus estimate; the differential part of the proof of original Theorem 5.1.</p><p>Use the marked data of Proposition 7, and let \\(h\\geq1\\), \\(s\\geq h\\), \\(F/F_0\\) finite with \\([F:F_0]_p\\leq p^h\\), and \\(S=FF'\\). Let \\(Z\\) normalize \\(E_F\\) in a purely inseparable function-field extension of degree \\(d\\).</p><p>Then \\(Z_S\\) is integral and regular, and has degree \\(d\\) over \\(E_S\\). If \\(r_i\\) is its ramification index over \\(t_i\\), then\n\\[\n\\frac{\\nu(Z)}d\n\\geq\n\\sum_{i=1}^n\\left(1-\\frac1{r_i}\\right)\n-\\bigl(h+\\log_p d\\bigr).\n\\tag{5.15}\n\\]\nHere \\(\\nu(Z)\\) is computed over \\(F\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Normalization is finite, and normal curves over fields are regular. A finite purely inseparable normalization map is radicial: locally a fixed \\(p^v\\)th power of each upstairs element lies downstairs, by normality. After any base change, membership in a prime is determined by this power, giving unique primes and purely inseparable residue extensions.</p><p>The extension \\(S/F\\) is finite separable. Thus \\(Z_S\\) is regular and reduced; its finite surjective radicial map to integral \\(E_S\\) makes it irreducible, hence integral. It is the normalization in its function field, and degree remains \\(d\\) by finite flatness. Proposition 9 gives \\(\\nu(Z_S)=\\nu(Z)\\). The case \\(d=1\\) is immediate.</p><p>Write \\(M_e=S(E)\\), \\(M_z=S(Z)\\), and form\n\\[\nM_j=M_eM_z^{p^j},\n\\qquad\nM_0=M_z,\\quad M_b=M_e,\n\\]\nwhere \\(b\\) is the first terminal index. Let \\(Y_j\\) normalize \\(E_S\\) in \\(M_j\\), and put\n\\[\n\\gamma_j:Y_j\\to E_S,\n\\quad\nd_j=[M_j:M_e],\n\\quad\n\\pi_j:Y_j\\to Y_{j+1},\n\\quad\nq_j=d_j/d_{j+1}=p^{m_j}.\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\nM_{j+1}=M_eM_j^p,\n\\tag{5.12}\n\\]\nProposition 10 applies to every step.</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal F_j\\) be the tangent kernel of \\(\\pi_j\\). It is also the kernel of the tangent map to \\(E_S\\): at function fields, a derivation kills \\(M_e\\) exactly when it kills \\(M_eM_j^p\\), and the equality extends as sheaves because the targets are torsion-free.</p><p>Choose an invariant differential \\(\\eta\\). The splitting\n\\[\n\\Omega_{E_S/\\kappa}\n\\simeq\n\\mathcal O_{E_S}\\eta\n\\oplus\n(\\mathcal O_{E_S}\\otimes_S\\Omega_{S/\\kappa})\n\\]\nmakes its tangent bundle trivial of rank \\(n+1\\). Let \\(\\mathcal H_j\\) be the tangent-map image in \\(\\gamma_j^*T_{E_S}\\), and put\n\\[\nb_j^\\circ=-\\deg_S\\mathcal H_j.\n\\]\nThe image is locally free of rank \\(n+1-m_j\\). Proposition 10 and the kernel-image sequence give\n\\[\n\\nu(Y_j)=q_j\\nu(Y_{j+1})+(p-1)\\deg_S\\mathcal F_j,\n\\]\n\\[\nb_j^\\circ=\\nu(Y_j)+\\deg_S\\mathcal F_j.\n\\]\nEliminating the kernel degree yields\n\\[\n\\frac{\\nu(Y_j)}{d_j}\n=\n\\frac1p\\frac{\\nu(Y_{j+1})}{d_{j+1}}\n+\n\\left(1-\\frac1p\\right)\\frac{b_j^\\circ}{d_j}.\n\\tag{5.13}\n\\]\nThe terminal genus term is zero.</p><p>Let \\(S_j\\) be the labels ramified for \\(\\gamma_j\\). For \\(i\\in S_j\\), put\n\\[\nD_{j,i}=\\gamma_j^*(t_i),\n\\qquad\nv_i=(1,-t_i^*\\eta).\n\\]\nThe functional \\(v_i\\) on \\(\\mathcal H_j\\) vanishes along the full divisor \\(D_{j,i}\\). Indeed, for a parameter \\(a\\) at \\(t_i\\),\n\\[\nda\\equiv\\lambda_i v_i\\pmod{a\\Omega_{E_S/\\kappa}},\n\\qquad\n\\lambda_i\\in S^\\times.\n\\]\nUpstairs write \\(a=u\\tau^r\\). Since the nontrivial purely inseparable index is divisible by \\(p\\),\n\\[\nd(\\gamma_j^*a)=\\tau^rdu=(\\gamma_j^*a)u^{-1}du.\n\\]\nPullback of \\(v_i\\) is consequently divisible by the full pullback divisor. Pairing with tangent vectors proves the assertion.</p><p>By Proposition 8 choose \\(c\\geq|S_j|-h\\) independent such covectors and complete them to a constant basis. Then\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_j\n\\hookrightarrow\n\\mathcal O_{Y_j}^{\\,n+1-c}\n\\oplus\n\\bigoplus_{i\\text{ chosen}}\\mathcal O_{Y_j}(-D_{j,i}).\n\\tag{5.14}\n\\]\nTaking the exterior power of rank \\(n+1-m_j\\), a nonzero component of the determinant map uses at least \\(c-m_j\\) negative summands when this is positive. Each has degree \\(-d_j\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\frac{b_j^\\circ}{d_j}\\geq |S_j|-h-m_j.\n\\]</p><p>Write \\(r_i=p^{u_i}\\). Each height-one step has local index at most \\(p\\), so \\(i\\in S_j\\) whenever \\(j&lt;u_i\\). Iterating (5.13), with\n\\[\nw_j=p^{-j}(1-1/p),\n\\]\ngives\n\\[\n\\frac{\\nu(Z)}d=\\sum_{j=0}^{b-1}w_j\\frac{b_j^\\circ}{d_j}.\n\\]\nFor each label,\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=0}^{u_i-1}w_j=1-\\frac1{r_i}.\n\\]\nHence the label contributions are at least the sum in (5.15). Since \\(m_j\\leq\\log_p d\\) and \\(\\sum_jw_j&lt;1\\), the total error is at most \\(h+\\log_p d\\). This proves (5.15).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Divisibility of the genus discrepancy; the divisor part of the proof of original Theorem 5.1.", "statement": "<p>Divisibility of the genus discrepancy; the divisor part of the proof of original Theorem 5.1.</p><p>Use Proposition 11's assumptions, and put \\(N=p^{s-h}\\). Then\n\\[\n\\nu(Z)\\in N\\mathbb Z,\n\\qquad\nd\\sum_{i=1}^n\\left(1-\\frac1{r_i}\\right)\\in N\\mathbb Z.\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\nA=\n\\nu(Z)-d\\sum_{i=1}^n\\left(1-\\frac1{r_i}\\right)\n\\]\nis a multiple of \\(N\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>By Proposition 9, \\(\\nu(Z)\\) is the degree over \\(F\\) of \\(\\det\\Omega_{Z/\\kappa}\\). A rational trivialization gives a divisor on \\(Z\\); its pushforward to \\(E_F\\) preserves degree. Proposition 8 therefore makes \\(\\nu(Z)\\) divisible by \\(N\\), including any inseparable constant-field contribution.</p><p>Let \\(T\\) be the reduced inverse image of \\(B_F\\) on \\(Z\\). It is defined over \\(F\\). Finite separable scalar extension preserves reducedness. If \\(z_i\\) is the unique point over \\(t_i\\), finite flatness gives\n\\[\nr_i[\\kappa(z_i):S]=d.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n\\deg_F T=\\deg_S T_S=\\sum_i\\frac d{r_i}.\n\\]\nFor \\(\\gamma:Z\\to E_F\\), the divisor \\(\\gamma^*B_F-T\\) has degree\n\\[\ndn-\\sum_i\\frac d{r_i}\n=\nd\\sum_i\\left(1-\\frac1{r_i}\\right).\n\\]\nPushforward preserves its degree, and Proposition 8 gives divisibility by \\(N\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "Proposition 6.1, Lifting the twist and its marked points", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fix the data of Proposition 7 and a positive integer \\(m\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix the data of Proposition 7 and a positive integer \\(m\\). There are complete discretely valued fields \\(K_b\\subset K_b'\\) of characteristic zero, valuation rings \\(R_b\\subset R_b'\\), and a smooth projective genus-one curve \\(\\mathcal X/R_b\\) such that:</p><p>\\[\nK_b'/K_b\n\\]\nis cyclic Galois of degree \\(n\\), \\(R_b'/R_b\\) is finite étale Galois, its residue extension is \\(F'/F_0\\), and the value groups agree. The field \\(K_b\\) contains \\(\\mathbb Q_p\\) and all \\(m\\)th roots of unity.</p><p>The special fiber is \\(E/F_0\\), and \\(\\mathcal X\\) has a relatively ample effective divisor \\(D\\) of fiber degree \\(n\\).</p><p>Over \\(R_b'\\), pairwise disjoint sections \\(\\xi_1,\\ldots,\\xi_n\\) reduce to \\(t_1,\\ldots,t_n\\), avoid \\(D\\), and have union descending to a finite étale divisor on \\(\\mathcal X\\). Descent permutes them by \\(\\xi_i\\mapsto\\xi_{i+1}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Starting with \\(\\mathbb Z_p\\), lift monic separable irreducible residue polynomials through a tower exhausting \\(\\kappa\\). Each lifted quotient is finite free, local with the original uniformizer, and a domain: a monic fraction-field factorization would have integral coefficients and contradict irreducibility of the reduction. It is therefore a DVR, with derivative a unit. The union has value group \\(\\mathbb Z\\) and residue \\(\\kappa\\); completion retains both. Denote the resulting constant DVR provisionally by \\(R_c^{(0)}\\).</p><p>Lift a smooth Weierstrass equation for \\(A_0\\). Its unit discriminant gives an elliptic scheme \\(\\mathcal A\\). The finite flat scheme \\(\\mathcal A[n]\\) has dense generic fiber: its uniformizer is a nonzerodivisor, so no component is supported in the special fiber. One of its finitely many generic points has closure containing \\(P\\). After a finite extension and passage to the integral closure of \\(R_c^{(0)}\\), lying over gives an \\(n\\)-torsion section \\(\\widetilde P\\) specializing to \\(P\\). Its order is exactly \\(n\\). Enlarge the constant field to contain the \\(m\\)th roots of unity. Its residue remains \\(\\kappa\\). Write \\(R_c\\) for the resulting complete DVR and \\(\\varpi\\) for its uniformizer.</p><p>At the generic point of the special fiber of \\(\\mathcal A^n/R_c\\), the local ring is a DVR with uniformizer \\(\\varpi\\) and residue \\(F'\\). Complete its fraction field to obtain \\(K_b'\\). The projections give points \\(\\xi_i\\), extending by properness to sections over \\(R_b'\\).</p><p>The product automorphism\n\\[\n\\sigma(\\xi_i)=\\xi_{i+1}-\\widetilde P\n\\]\nhas order \\(n\\), preserves the valuation, and extends to \\(K_b'\\). Put \\(K_b=(K_b')^{\\langle\\sigma\\rangle}\\). It is closed and complete. The fixed uniformizer gives the same value group. Fixed-field theory gives degree \\(n\\). If \\(k_b\\) is its residue, then \\(k_b\\subseteq F_0\\), while lifting residue-independent elements gives\n\\[\nn=[F':F_0]\\leq[F':k_b]\\leq[K_b':K_b]=n.\n\\]\nThus \\(k_b=F_0\\).</p><p>Lift a primitive residue element \\(\\bar a\\) of \\(F'/F_0\\) to \\(a\\in R_b'\\). The polynomial\n\\[\n\\prod_{j=0}^{n-1}(T-\\sigma^ja)\\in R_b[T]\n\\]\nhas separable irreducible reduction. Hence \\(R_b[a]\\) is a DVR, finite free of rank \\(n\\), with derivative a unit. Uniqueness of the complete-DVR valuation identifies it with \\(R_b'\\). The Galois torsor map is an isomorphism after reduction and hence an isomorphism of these free modules. Thus \\(R_b'/R_b\\) is finite étale Galois.</p><p>On \\(\\mathcal A_{R_b'}\\), use\n\\[\n\\delta(x)=\\sigma(x)+\\widetilde P.\n\\]\nIts invariant divisor\n\\[\nD'=\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}[j\\widetilde P]\n\\]\nhas disjoint sections and a canonically linearized ample line bundle. Finite étale Galois descent gives \\(\\mathcal X,D\\); their reduction is the twist \\(E\\) and its origin-orbit divisor. The \\(\\xi_i\\) have distinct reductions, avoid \\(D'\\), and satisfy \\(\\delta(\\xi_i)=\\xi_{i+1}\\). Their invariant disjoint union descends as a finite étale divisor.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "Proposition 6.2, Spherical base and finite extensions", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is an extension \\(K_s/K_b\\), for the field in Proposition 13, with value group \\(\\mathbb Q\\), residue field \\(F_0\\), and spherical completeness.", "statement": "<p>There is an extension \\(K_s/K_b\\), for the field in Proposition 13, with value group \\(\\mathbb Q\\), residue field \\(F_0\\), and spherical completeness.</p><p>Every finite extension \\(L/K_s\\), with an extended valuation, has value group \\(\\mathbb Q\\), is spherically complete, and satisfies\n\\[\n[\\operatorname{res}(L):F_0]=[L:K_s].\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\nThe valuation on \\(K_s\\), and on each such \\(L\\), extends uniquely to every algebraic extension. As abstract fields,\n\\[\n\\overline{K_s}\\simeq\\mathbb C.\n\\]</p><p>A valued field is spherically complete if every nested family of nonempty closed balls has nonempty intersection; an extension is immediate if neither its value group nor residue field changes.</p>", "proof": "<p>Normalize \\(v(\\varpi)=1\\). Choose compatible factorial roots with\n\\[\n\\theta_1=\\varpi,\n\\qquad\n\\theta_{j+1}^{j+1}=\\theta_j,\n\\qquad\n\\theta_j^{j!}=\\varpi.\n\\]\nFor \\(r=j!\\), the powers \\(1,\\theta_j,\\ldots,\\theta_j^{r-1}\\) have distinct value cosets modulo \\(\\mathbb Z\\), so form a basis of \\(K_b(\\theta_j)\\), with\n\\[\nv\\left(\\sum_{a=0}^{r-1}c_a\\theta_j^a\\right)\n=\n\\min_a\\{v(c_a)+a/r\\}.\n\\]\nThe value group is \\(r^{-1}\\mathbb Z\\), and residue remains \\(F_0\\). Thus\n\\[\nK_\\infty=\\bigcup_jK_b(\\theta_j)\n\\]\nhas value group \\(\\mathbb Q\\) and residue \\(F_0\\).</p><p>A valued field with countable value group and countable residue has cardinality at most \\(\\mathfrak c=2^{\\aleph_0}\\). For each closed ball of rational radius, label its open subballs by natural numbers. Recording a point's open-subball label for every radius distinguishes points, giving an injection into a countable product of countable sets. The residue \\(F_0\\) is countable. On a fixed set of cardinality greater than \\(\\mathfrak c\\), Zorn's lemma therefore gives a maximal immediate extension \\(K_s\\); any further one could be relabeled into the unused set.</p><p>To prove spherical completeness, an empty nest has a countable cofinal subnest, giving centers and increasing values\n\\[\nv(a_j-a_i)=\\gamma_i\\quad(j&gt;i),\n\\qquad\n\\gamma_1&lt;\\gamma_2&lt;\\cdots,\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]\nwhose associated balls have empty intersection. A pseudo-limit satisfies \\(v(b-a_i)=\\gamma_i\\) eventually. A point that is not a pseudo-limit has eventually constant values \\(v(b-a_i)&lt;\\gamma_i\\).</p><p>If an algebraic pseudo-limit \\(\\alpha\\) exists, choose one of minimum degree \\(d\\). Every root of a polynomial of degree less than \\(d\\) is not a pseudo-limit. Factoring such a polynomial \\(f\\) gives, for large \\(i\\),\n\\[\nv(f(\\alpha)-f(a_i))&gt;v(f(\\alpha)).\n\\tag{6.3}\n\\]\nEvery element of \\(K_s(\\alpha)\\) has a representative polynomial of degree less than \\(d\\), so this is a proper immediate extension, a contradiction.</p><p>If no algebraic pseudo-limit exists, every nonzero rational function has eventually constant value on the \\(a_i\\). Set\n\\[\nw(f)=\\text{the eventual value of }v(f(a_i))\n\\]\non \\(K_s(T)\\). Evaluation gives a valuation. Factoring numerator and denominator shows, for sufficiently large \\(i\\),\n\\[\nw(f-f(a_i))&gt;w(f).\n\\]\nThus \\(K_s(T)\\) is a proper immediate extension, again a contradiction. Hence \\(K_s\\) is spherically complete.</p><p>The polynomial minimum-value argument in Lemma 14.2 shows that every algebraic extension has value group \\(\\mathbb Q\\). Apply Lemma 14.1 to \\(L\\) as a normed \\(K_s\\)-space. Its integral and positive balls are its valuation ring and maximal ideal, so\n\\[\n[\\operatorname{res}(L):F_0]=[L:K_s],\n\\]\nand \\(L\\) is spherically complete. The same holds for extensions of \\(L\\).</p><p>If a finite \\(U/L\\) had two distinct prolongations, each would have residue degree \\([U:L]\\), contradicting Lemma 14.2. Existence and restriction to finite subextensions give uniqueness on all algebraic extensions.</p><p>Finally, \\(|K_s|\\leq\\mathfrak c\\), while \\(\\mathbb Q_p\\subset K_s\\) gives the reverse inequality. Its algebraic closure has cardinality \\(\\mathfrak c\\), hence transcendence degree \\(\\mathfrak c\\) over \\(\\mathbb Q\\). Classification of algebraically closed fields gives \\(\\overline{K_s}\\simeq\\mathbb C\\), with no asserted topological compatibility.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(K_*\\) be spherically complete with value group \\(\\mathbb Q\\), valuation ring \\(R_*\\), maximal ideal \\(\\mathfrak m_*\\), and residue \\(k_*\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(K_*\\) be spherically complete with value group \\(\\mathbb Q\\), valuation ring \\(R_*\\), maximal ideal \\(\\mathfrak m_*\\), and residue \\(k_*\\). Let \\(V\\) be a finite-dimensional \\(K_*\\)-space with valuation norm\n\\[\n\\lambda:V\\to\\mathbb Q\\cup\\{+\\infty\\},\n\\]\nfinite off zero, satisfying the strong triangle inequality and\n\\[\n\\lambda(ax)=v(a)+\\lambda(x).\n\\]\nThere is a basis \\(e_1,\\ldots,e_d\\) of norm zero with\n\\[\n\\lambda\\left(\\sum_i a_ie_i\\right)=\\min_i v(a_i).\n\\tag{6.4}\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\nV_{\\geq0}=\\bigoplus_iR_*e_i,\n\\quad\nV_{&gt;0}=\\mathfrak m_*V_{\\geq0},\n\\quad\n\\dim_{k_*}V_{\\geq0}/V_{&gt;0}=d.\n\\tag{6.5}\n\\]\nThe normed space \\(V\\) is spherically complete.</p>", "proof": "<p>A subspace with an orthogonal basis is spherically complete because its balls are finite products of coordinate balls. Suppose \\(U\\) has such a basis and \\(x\\notin U\\). For every achieved value \\(\\gamma=\\lambda(x-u)\\), consider\n\\[\nB_\\gamma=\\{u\\in U:\\lambda(x-u)\\geq\\gamma\\}.\n\\]\nThese nonempty closed balls form a nest. Their intersection contains \\(u_0\\), a best approximation. Put \\(y=x-u_0\\) and \\(\\ell=\\lambda(y)\\). Maximality and the strong triangle law give\n\\[\n\\lambda(y+u)=\\min\\{\\ell,\\lambda(u)\\}\n\\quad(u\\in U).\n\\]\nHomogeneity permits adjoining \\(y\\) orthogonally. Induction gives an orthogonal basis, and its norms can be scaled to zero because they lie in the base value group. The formulas follow; the coordinate argument also gives spherical completeness.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.2", "orig": "Lemma 6.4, Simultaneous residue approximation", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(T\\) be nontrivially valued with value group \\(\\mathbb Q\\) and infinite residue field \\(k_T\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(T\\) be nontrivially valued with value group \\(\\mathbb Q\\) and infinite residue field \\(k_T\\). Let \\(U/T\\) be finite, and let \\(w_1,\\ldots,w_r\\) be distinct prolongations with residues \\(k_1,\\ldots,k_r\\). Then\n\\[\n\\bigcap_{i=1}^r\\mathcal O_{w_i}\n\\longrightarrow\n\\prod_{i=1}^rk_i\n\\]\nis surjective, and\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^r[k_i:k_T]\\leq[U:T].\n\\tag{6.6}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>A polynomial relation for an algebraic element has at least two terms of equal minimum value. Thus an integer multiple of its value lies in \\(\\mathbb Q\\); divisibility and torsion-freeness put its value in \\(\\mathbb Q\\).</p><p>For \\(i\\ne j\\), distinctness of valuations, inversion, and multiplication by a scalar of suitable rational value give \\(z\\in U\\) with\n\\[\nw_i(z)&gt;0&gt;w_j(z).\n\\]\nChoose a unit \\(c\\in T\\) so that \\(1+cz\\) has nonzero residue at every \\(w_\\ell\\) where \\(w_\\ell(z)=0\\). Each excludes at most one residue of \\(c\\), so infinitude permits the choice. Then\n\\[\nq_{ij}=(1+cz)^{-1}\n\\]\nis integral everywhere, has residue one at \\(i\\), and zero at \\(j\\). Put \\(q_i=\\prod_{j\\ne i}q_{ij}\\). It has residue one at \\(i\\) and positive value elsewhere.</p><p>For \\(b_i\\in k_i\\), choose a lift \\(\\widetilde b_i\\) integral at \\(i\\), and choose \\(N_i\\) large enough that\n\\[\nN_iw_\\ell(q_i)+w_\\ell(\\widetilde b_i)&gt;0\n\\quad(\\ell\\ne i).\n\\]\nThen \\(q_i^{N_i}\\widetilde b_i\\) has the desired residue in coordinate \\(i\\) and zero elsewhere. Summation proves surjectivity.</p><p>Each residue degree is finite by the single-place lifting argument. Lift bases of all \\(k_i/k_T\\) with zero residues in the other coordinates. A nonzero \\(T\\)-linear relation, divided by a coefficient of minimum value, reduces in a coordinate containing a unit coefficient to a contradiction. These lifts are linearly independent, proving (6.6).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "Proposition 6.6, Descent from a small Sylow orbit", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Fix a finite group \\(J\\) with \\(O_p(J)=1\\), put \\(m=|J|\\), and use Propositions 13 and 14 with this \\(m\\).", "statement": "<p>Fix a finite group \\(J\\) with \\(O_p(J)=1\\), put \\(m=|J|\\), and use Propositions 13 and 14 with this \\(m\\). Choose\n\\[\nK_b'\\hookrightarrow\\overline{K_s},\n\\qquad\n\\overline{K_s}\\simeq\\mathbb C,\n\\]\nand write \\(X_s=\\mathcal X_{K_s}\\).</p><p>A marked \\(J\\)-cover is a connected smooth projective cover of the geometric base, Galois of group \\(J\\), with a specified left \\(J\\)-action. Isomorphisms are over the fixed base and commute with that action.</p><p>For a multiset \\(\\mathcal C\\) of \\(n=p^s\\) conjugacy classes of \\(p\\)-singular elements, let \\(\\mathcal T_{\\mathcal C}\\) be the finite set of marked-cover classes branched only at the \\(\\xi_i\\) and having this multiset of local classes.</p><p>The Galois action on \\(\\mathcal T_{\\mathcal C}\\) factors through a finite Galois extension \\(M/K_s\\). Put \\(\\Gamma=\\operatorname{Gal}(M/K_s)\\), and let \\(P_\\Gamma\\) be a Sylow \\(p\\)-subgroup. If a \\(P_\\Gamma\\)-orbit has size less than \\(p^h\\), one of its covers descends to a finite extension \\(L/K_s\\) with\n\\[\n[L:K_s]_p&lt;p^h,\n\\qquad\n[F:F_0]_p=[L:K_s]_p,\n\\qquad\nF=\\operatorname{res}(L).\n\\tag{6.7}\n\\]\nThe descended cover has geometrically connected smooth projective source, degree \\(m\\), specified \\(J\\)-action, and all marked branch indices divisible by \\(p\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose representatives of the finitely many cover classes. Their equations, maps, and deck actions are finite data and are defined over a finite Galois \\(N/K_s\\), enlarged to contain \\(K_sK_b'\\). For every element of \\(\\operatorname{Gal}(N/K_s)\\), choose marked isomorphisms from conjugates to the chosen representatives of their classes. Enclose their finitely many coefficients in a finite Galois \\(M/K_s\\). All needed isomorphisms are then defined over \\(M\\), and the class action factors through \\(\\Gamma\\).</p><p>Let \\(S_p\\leq P_\\Gamma\\) stabilize a class in a small orbit. For its model \\(Y_M\\), form the finite group \\(\\mathcal E\\) of marked semilinear isomorphisms for all scalar actions in \\(S_p\\). It fits into\n\\[\n1\\to Z(J)\\to\\mathcal E\\to S_p\\to1.\n\\tag{6.8}\n\\]\nIndeed, automorphisms over the base are the specified deck transformations, and those commuting with the action are exactly \\(Z(J)\\). This kernel is central.</p><p>The Sylow \\(p\\)-subgroup of \\(Z(J)\\) is characteristic there and normal in \\(J\\); \\(O_p(J)=1\\) makes it trivial. Hence \\(p\\nmid |Z(J)|\\). A Sylow \\(p\\)-subgroup of \\(\\mathcal E\\) maps isomorphically to \\(S_p\\), giving compatible semilinear maps and therefore a descent datum.</p><p>The pullback of an ample line bundle on the base has a compatible linearization. Projective Galois descent gives the cover over \\(L=M^{S_p}\\), with the specified action. Its geometric properties are checked after extension to \\(\\overline{K_s}\\). Finally,\n\\[\n[L:K_s]_p\n=\n[\\Gamma:S_p]_p\n=\n|P_\\Gamma:S_p|\n&lt;\np^h.\n\\]\nProposition 14 gives the equality of full field and residue degrees, hence (6.7).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After choosing oriented handle and puncture generators on the complex punctured base, marked \\(J\\)-cover classes correspond bijectively to …", "statement": "<p>After choosing oriented handle and puncture generators on the complex punctured base, marked \\(J\\)-cover classes correspond bijectively to\n\\[\n\\left\\{\n(x,y,u_1,\\ldots,u_n)\\in J^{n+2}:\n[x,y]u_1\\cdots u_n=1,\\ \n\\langle x,y,u_1,\\ldots,u_n\\rangle=J\n\\right\\}/J,\n\\]\nwith simultaneous inner conjugation. The inertia order at \\(\\xi_i\\) is \\(|u_i|\\). The set \\(\\mathcal T_{\\mathcal C}\\) is Galois-stable, and\n\\[\n|\\mathcal T_{\\mathcal C}|=N_{J,s}(\\mathcal C).\n\\]\nThe puncture entries remain ordered; specifying their class multiset does not quotient by permutations.</p>", "proof": "<p>In characteristic zero the base is a Weierstrass cubic whose completed-square equation is a double cover of the line branched at three roots and infinity. Cutting and crosswise gluing identifies its surface as a torus.</p><p>The punctured fundamental group has generators \\(x,y,u_1,\\ldots,u_n\\) and relation\n\\[\n[x,y]u_1\\cdots u_n=1.\n\\]\nChoosing a fiber point identifies the fiber with the regular left \\(J\\)-set. Path lifting commutes with deck action, so monodromy is by right translations. Connectedness is surjectivity of the resulting homomorphism to \\(J\\). Changing the chosen fiber point conjugates it; equivariant fiber isomorphisms give exactly simultaneous inner conjugacy, not outer automorphisms.</p><p>Riemann existence algebraizes these unramified covers and their actions. Normalization of the projective base completes them; normal complex curves are smooth. A local chart \\(z\\mapsto z^e\\) shows that local monodromy order equals ramification index.</p><p>For each \\(e\\mid m\\), choose \\(\\zeta_e\\in K_s\\) mapping to \\(\\exp(2\\pi i/e)\\). At a point above the puncture, its local class is the conjugacy class of the unique inertia generator acting on the tangent line by \\(\\zeta_e\\). This agrees with a positive puncture loop, using the approach path and its translated return path. Galois fixes \\(\\zeta_e\\), so preserves the local class at the transported branch point. It permutes the \\(\\xi_i\\), whose union is defined over \\(K_s\\), and therefore preserves the prescribed multiset. Finiteness follows from the finite tuple set.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 16", "orig": "Lemma 6.7, Matching branch permutations after reduction", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(L/K_s\\) be finite, let \\(F=\\operatorname{res}(L)\\), and put \\(L'=LK_b'\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(L/K_s\\) be finite, let \\(F=\\operatorname{res}(L)\\), and put \\(L'=LK_b'\\). In \\(\\operatorname{res}(L')\\), form \\(S=FF'\\).</p><p>Then \\(L'/L\\) and \\(S/F\\) are finite Galois, \\(\\operatorname{res}(L')=S\\), and reduction gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Gal}(L'/L)\\simeq\\operatorname{Gal}(S/F).\n\\]\nThe permutation actions on \\(\\xi_1,\\ldots,\\xi_n\\) and \\(t_1,\\ldots,t_n\\) agree. An invariant subset of indices therefore defines branch subsets over \\(L\\) and \\(F\\), respectively.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 14 gives a unique valuation on \\(L'\\); its restriction to \\(K_b'\\) is the original one by complete-DVR uniqueness. Put \\(T=\\operatorname{res}(L')\\). It contains \\(S\\). Base change makes \\(L'/L\\) Galois, and restriction embeds\n\\[\nH=\\operatorname{Gal}(L'/L)\n\\]\nin \\(\\operatorname{Gal}(K_b'/K_b)\\).</p><p>Reduction acts faithfully on \\(S\\): an element acting trivially on \\(S\\) acts trivially on \\(F'\\), hence on \\(K_b'\\) by its faithful residue action, and then on \\(L'\\). Since \\(S/F\\) is Galois,\n\\[\n|H|\\leq[S:F]\\leq[T:F]\\leq[L':L]=|H|.\n\\tag{6.9}\n\\]\nThus all inequalities are equalities.</p><p>The sections extend to the valuation ring of \\(L'\\), reduce to the distinct \\(t_i\\), and their Galois actions commute with reduction. Hence the permutations agree. Invariant subsets descend along the corresponding finite Galois extensions.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 17", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Residue curves, total degree, and inseparable quotient; original Lemma 7.4 and Sections 7.1–7.2.", "statement": "<p>Residue curves, total degree, and inseparable quotient; original Lemma 7.4 and Sections 7.1–7.2.</p><p>Suppose \\(L/K_s\\) is finite, with valuation ring \\(R\\), maximal ideal \\(\\mathfrak m_R\\), and residue \\(F\\). Suppose\n\\[\nf:Y\\to X=\\mathcal X_L\n\\]\nis a geometrically connected smooth projective \\(J\\)-Galois cover of degree \\(m=|J|\\), with specified \\(J\\)-action over \\(L\\), branched only at the marked points. Write \\(D_X\\) and \\(D_F\\) for the degree-\\(n\\) base divisors and \\(D_Y=f^*D_X\\).</p><p>There is a Gauss valuation on \\(L(X)\\) with value group \\(\\mathbb Q\\) and residue \\(F(E_F)\\). Choose one prolongation to \\(L(Y)\\), and let \\(\\mathcal V\\) be its \\(J\\)-orbit. For \\(w\\in\\mathcal V\\), let \\(K(w)\\) be its residue, let \\(C_w\\) normalize \\(E_F\\) in \\(K(w)\\), and put\n\\[\nm_w=[K(w):F(E_F)].\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\sum_{w\\in\\mathcal V}m_w=m.\n\\tag{7.8}\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(\\Delta_w\\leq J\\) stabilize \\(w\\), and let \\(I_w\\) be the kernel of its action on \\(K(w)\\). Then\n\\[\nm_w=|\\Delta_w|,\n\\tag{7.9}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nM_w=K(w)^{\\Delta_w}\n\\]\nis purely inseparable over \\(F(E_F)\\), of degree\n\\[\nd=[M_w:F(E_F)]=|I_w|.\n\\tag{7.10}\n\\]\nThus \\(d\\) is a power of \\(p\\) and \\(d\\leq m\\). If \\(Z_w\\) normalizes \\(E_F\\) in \\(M_w\\), then \\(C_w\\to Z_w\\) is generically separable Galois of degree \\(m_w/d\\), with group \\(\\Delta_w/I_w\\). These towers are isomorphic over \\(E_F\\) as \\(w\\) varies.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 17.1 constructs the Gauss valuation. Residue degrees are finite by Lemma 14.2. The normalizations \\(C_w\\) are regular integral projective curves, finite over \\(E_F\\).</p><p>For large \\(j\\), Lemma 17.2, Proposition 9, and ample vanishing give\n\\[\njmn+\\chi(Y)\n\\leq\njn\\sum_wm_w+\\sum_w\\chi(C_w).\n\\tag{7.7}\n\\]\nLetting \\(j\\) grow gives \\(m\\leq\\sum_wm_w\\). Lemma 14.2 applied to these prolongations of the Gauss valuation gives the reverse inequality, hence (7.8). The orbit has \\(m/|\\Delta_w|\\) members of equal residue degree, proving (7.9).</p><p>For \\(x\\in K(w)^{\\Delta_w}\\), simultaneous approximation gives \\(a\\in L(Y)\\), integral at every member of \\(\\mathcal V\\), with residue \\(x\\) at \\(w\\) and zero elsewhere. At \\(w\\), the reduction of (7.6) is\n\\[\n\\overline{P_a}(T)\n=\nT^{m-|\\Delta_w|}(T-x)^{|\\Delta_w|}\n\\in F(E_F)[T].\n\\tag{7.11}\n\\]\nWrite \\(|\\Delta_w|=p^vq\\) with \\(p\\nmid q\\). The coefficient of \\(T^{m-p^v}\\) is \\(-qx^{p^v}\\). Hence \\(x^{p^v}\\in F(E_F)\\), proving pure inseparability.</p><p>Finite fixed-field theory gives\n\\[\n[K(w):M_w]=|\\Delta_w/I_w|.\n\\]\nTogether with \\(m_w=|\\Delta_w|\\), the tower formula gives (7.10). The remaining assertions follow by normalization and the conjugating action of \\(J\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 17.1", "orig": "Lemma 7.2, Gauss valuation and reduction of sections", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In Proposition 17's setting, there is a valuation \\(v_X\\) on \\(L(X)\\), extending that on \\(L\\), with value group \\(\\mathbb Q\\) and residue \\(F(E_F)\\).", "statement": "<p>In Proposition 17's setting, there is a valuation \\(v_X\\) on \\(L(X)\\), extending that on \\(L\\), with value group \\(\\mathbb Q\\) and residue \\(F(E_F)\\). For all sufficiently large \\(j\\), its integral ball in\n\\[\nH^0(X,\\mathcal O_X(jD_X))\n\\]\nreduces onto\n\\[\nH^0(E_F,\\mathcal O_{E_F}(jD_F)).\n\\]\nReduction respects inclusions and multiplication. Over \\(L'=LK_b'\\), whose residue is \\(S=FF'\\), an integral section \\(b\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\overline{b(\\xi_i)}=\\bar b(t_i)\\quad\\text{in }S.\n\\tag{7.3}\n\\]\nIn particular, generic vanishing implies reduced vanishing.</p>", "proof": "<p>Over \\(R_b\\), ample vanishing and the special-fiber sequence give, for large \\(j\\),\n\\[\nH^0(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal O(jD))\\otimes_{R_b}F_0\n\\simeq\nH^0(E,\\mathcal O_E(jD_E)).\n\\]\nThe module before reduction is finite torsion-free, hence free. Choose a basis whose reductions are a special-fiber basis. Flat base change gives bases over \\(L\\) and \\(F\\).</p><p>Regarding sections as rational functions, define\n\\[\nv_X\\left(\\sum_\\alpha c_\\alpha b_{j,\\alpha}\\right)\n=\n\\min_\\alpha v(c_\\alpha).\n\\]\nAn integral section of minimum zero has nonzero reduction. Integral inclusions between the section spaces preserve this fact, so the definition is independent of the large space used. Nonzero reductions multiply nontrivially because \\(E_F\\) is integral; this proves multiplicativity. The triangle inequality is immediate.</p><p>The union of the section spaces has fraction field \\(L(X)\\), since high ample powers give an embedding. Thus the rule extends to the desired valuation. Fractions of value zero reduce to ratios of special sections; conversely such ratios generate \\(F(E_F)\\). The value group is \\(\\mathbb Q\\), already supplied by \\(L\\).</p><p>The basis functions are regular along the split sections because these avoid \\(D\\), and their evaluations reduce to evaluations at \\(t_i\\). Proposition 16 identifies the residue as \\(S\\), proving (7.3).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 17.2", "orig": "Lemma 7.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "With \\(\\mathcal V,C_w\\) as in Proposition 17, let \\(D_w\\) be the pullback of \\(D_F\\), and put \\( V_j=H^0(Y,\\mathcal O_Y(jD_Y)), \\qquad v_{\\mathcal V}(a)=\\min_{w\\in\\mathcal V}w(a), \\) …", "statement": "<p>With \\(\\mathcal V,C_w\\) as in Proposition 17, let \\(D_w\\) be the pullback of \\(D_F\\), and put\n\\[\nV_j=H^0(Y,\\mathcal O_Y(jD_Y)),\n\\qquad\nv_{\\mathcal V}(a)=\\min_{w\\in\\mathcal V}w(a),\n\\]\n\\[\nA_j=\\{a\\in V_j:v_{\\mathcal V}(a)\\geq0\\}.\n\\]\nThen \\(A_j\\) is a finite free \\(R\\)-module of rank \\(\\dim_LV_j\\), its positive ball is \\(\\mathfrak m_RA_j\\), and for all sufficiently large \\(j\\) simultaneous residue gives an injection\n\\[\nA_j/\\mathfrak m_RA_j\n\\hookrightarrow\n\\bigoplus_{w\\in\\mathcal V}\nH^0(C_w,\\mathcal O_{C_w}(jD_w)).\n\\tag{7.5}\n\\]\nThese maps respect products.</p><p>If a section vanishes on reduced inverse images of a selected branch subset defined over the ground field, its reductions vanish on the corresponding reduced inverse images on the \\(C_w\\). This may be checked after splitting over \\(L'\\) and \\(S\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>All prolongation values lie in \\(\\mathbb Q\\) by algebraicity and divisibility of the base value group. The minimum is a valuation norm. Lemma 14.1 gives\n\\[\nA_j\\simeq R^{\\dim_LV_j},\n\\qquad\n\\{a:v_{\\mathcal V}(a)&gt;0\\}=\\mathfrak m_RA_j.\n\\tag{7.4}\n\\]\nThe same holds on every \\(L\\)-linear subspace. Simultaneous residue has exactly this kernel, so its image has dimension \\(\\dim_LV_j\\).</p><p>For \\(a\\in A_j\\), form\n\\[\nP_a(T)=\\prod_{g\\in J}(T-g(a))\n=\nT^m+c_1T^{m-1}+\\cdots+c_m.\n\\tag{7.6}\n\\]\nThe coefficients are invariant, hence lie in \\(L(X)\\). Every \\(g(a)\\) is integral at every \\(w\\), since \\(J\\) permutes \\(\\mathcal V\\), so \\(v_X(c_k)\\geq0\\). Their pole bounds give\n\\[\nc_k\\in H^0(X,\\mathcal O_X(kjD_X)).\n\\]\nIndeed the bound upstairs descends under finite surjective pullback. Lemma 17.1 gives the corresponding reduced pole bounds.</p><p>At a local equation \\(t\\) for \\(jD_F\\), the element \\(t\\bar a_w\\) satisfies a monic equation with regular coefficients \\(t^k\\bar c_k\\). Normality of \\(C_w\\) makes it regular. Hence \\(\\bar a_w\\) has the required poles, proving (7.5).</p><p>At a selected split marked point, every \\(g(a)\\) vanishes on its reduced inverse fiber. Thus the non-leading \\(c_k\\) vanish downstairs. Their reductions vanish at \\(t_i\\) by Lemma 17.1. Reducing the equation at an upstairs point gives \\(\\bar a_w^m=0\\) in its residue field, proving vanishing.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 18", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Ramification comparison and equality; original Sections 7.3–7.4.", "statement": "<p>Ramification comparison and equality; original Sections 7.3–7.4.</p><p>Assume Proposition 17's cover exists, assume \\(O_p(J)=1\\), and suppose\n\\[\n[L:K_s]_p&lt;p^h,\n\\qquad\ns\\geq\\max\\{1,h\\},\n\\qquad\np^{s-h}&gt;m(h+\\log_p m).\n\\tag{7.1}\n\\]\nLet \\(e_i\\) be the geometric ramification index of \\(Y\\to X\\) at \\(\\xi_i\\), and assume \\(p\\mid e_i\\) for every \\(i\\).</p><p>After \\(S=FF'\\), let \\(r_i\\) be the index of \\(Z_{w,S}\\to E_S\\) at \\(t_i\\), and let \\(a_i\\) be the index of \\(C_{w,S}\\to Z_{w,S}\\) above it. These indices are independent of the point and of \\(w\\), and\n\\[\nr_ia_i=e_i,\n\\qquad\np\\nmid a_i\n\\quad(1\\leq i\\leq n).\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\nd=|I_w|&gt;1,\n\\qquad\n\\chi(Y)=\\sum_{w\\in\\mathcal V}\\chi(C_w).\n\\tag{7.17}\n\\]\nAll Euler characteristics are over their stated ground fields.</p>", "proof": "<p>The source \\(Y\\) is geometrically integral: over an algebraic closure, smooth-curve components are disjoint, and connectedness leaves one. Proposition 14 gives\n\\[\n[F:F_0]_p=[L:K_s]_p&lt;p^h.\n\\]\nCharacteristic-zero Riemann–Hurwitz gives\n\\[\n\\frac{\\nu(Y)}m\n=\n\\sum_{i=1}^n\\left(1-\\frac1{e_i}\\right).\n\\tag{7.2}\n\\]</p><p>The curve \\(Z_{w,S}\\) remains regular integral because its map to \\(E_S\\) is finite surjective radicial and \\(S/F\\) is separable. The regular curve \\(C_{w,S}\\) is a disjoint union of integral components, each dominating \\(Z_{w,S}\\) by finite flatness.</p><p>Put \\(G_w=\\Delta_w/I_w\\). Its action on\n\\[\nK(w)\\otimes_{M_w}S(Z_{w,S})\n=\n\\prod_\\lambda K_\\lambda\n\\]\nis transitive on field factors, since the invariant algebra is the field \\(S(Z_{w,S})\\). A factor stabilizer has order equal to the factor degree and acts faithfully, so that factor extension is Galois. Transitivity on primes in Galois normalization, combined with transitivity on factors, gives transitivity on the entire fiber. Thus \\(a_i\\) is independent of the point. Conjugation between towers gives independence of \\(w\\).</p><p>Finite flatness gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{w\\in\\mathcal V\\\\P\\in C_{w,S},\\,P\\mid t_i}}\n[\\kappa(P):S]\n=\n\\frac m{r_ia_i}.\n\\tag{7.12}\n\\]</p><p>For \\(Z_w\\), Propositions 11 and 12 give a discrepancy \\(A\\) divisible by \\(p^{s-h}\\), with\n\\[\nA\\geq-d(h+\\log_p d)\n\\geq-m(h+\\log_p m)\n&gt;-p^{s-h}.\n\\]\nThus \\(A\\geq0\\), and\n\\[\n\\frac{\\nu(Z_w)}d\n\\geq\n\\sum_i\\left(1-\\frac1{r_i}\\right).\n\\]\nApply Proposition 9 componentwise to \\(C_{w,S}\\to Z_{w,S}\\), retaining the wild term:\n\\[\n\\nu(C_w)\n\\geq\n\\frac{m_w}{d}\\nu(Z_w)\n+\n\\sum_i\\frac{m_w}{r_ia_i}\n\\left(a_i-1+\\mathbf1_{p\\mid a_i}\\right).\n\\]\nUsing \\(\\sum_wm_w=m\\), obtain\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sum_w\\nu(C_w)}m\n\\geq\n\\sum_i\\left(1-\\frac1{r_i}\\right)\n+\n\\sum_i\\frac{a_i-1+\\mathbf1_{p\\mid a_i}}{r_ia_i}.\n\\tag{7.13}\n\\]</p><p>Select\n\\[\nI=\\left\\{i:\\frac1{r_ia_i}&gt;\\frac1{e_i}\\right\\}.\n\\]\nThe generic and special index data are constant on their respective label orbits. Proposition 16 identifies those permutation actions, so this subset descends on both sides.</p><p>Let \\(T_Y\\) and \\(T_w\\) be reduced inverse images of the selected branch divisors. Separable splitting preserves their degrees and reducedness, and\n\\[\n\\deg_LT_Y=\\sum_{i\\in I}\\frac m{e_i},\n\\qquad\n\\sum_w\\deg_FT_w=\\sum_{i\\in I}\\frac m{r_ia_i}.\n\\tag{7.14}\n\\]\nRestrict the norm to\n\\[\nH^0(Y,\\mathcal O_Y(jD_Y-T_Y)).\n\\]\nLemma 17.2 places its full-dimensional reduction in\n\\[\n\\bigoplus_wH^0(C_w,\\mathcal O_{C_w}(jD_w-T_w)).\n\\]\nFor large \\(j\\), degree equality and ample vanishing give\n\\[\n\\frac{\\nu(Y)}m\n\\geq\n\\frac{\\sum_w\\nu(C_w)}m\n+\n2\\sum_i\\max\\left\\{\\frac1{r_ia_i}-\\frac1{e_i},0\\right\\}.\n\\tag{7.15}\n\\]</p><p>Put\n\\[\nq_i=\\frac1{r_ia_i},\n\\qquad\nb_i=\\frac1{e_i},\n\\qquad\n\\delta_i=\\mathbf1_{p\\mid a_i}.\n\\]\nThe label contribution in (7.13) is \\(1-q_i+\\delta_iq_i\\). Combining (7.13), (7.15), and (7.2) yields\n\\[\n0\n\\geq\n\\sum_i\\left(b_i-q_i+2\\max\\{q_i-b_i,0\\}+\\delta_iq_i\\right)\n=\n\\sum_i\\left(|q_i-b_i|+\\delta_iq_i\\right)\n\\geq0.\n\\tag{7.16}\n\\]\nEvery term vanishes and all intervening inequalities are equalities. Thus \\(r_ia_i=e_i\\), \\(p\\nmid a_i\\), and the extra term in (7.15) is zero, giving the Euler-characteristic equality.</p><p>Since \\(p\\mid e_i\\) and \\(p\\nmid a_i\\), \\(r_i&gt;1\\). Its index divides the purely inseparable degree \\(d\\), so \\(d&gt;1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 19", "orig": "Proposition 7.5, A finitely presented model over the valuation ring", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Under Proposition 17's setting, assume \\( \\sum_wm_w=m, \\qquad \\chi(Y)=\\sum_w\\chi(C_w). \\) Then there is a flat projective \\(R\\)-scheme \\(\\mathcal Y\\) of finite presentation with …", "statement": "<p>Under Proposition 17's setting, assume\n\\[\n\\sum_wm_w=m,\n\\qquad\n\\chi(Y)=\\sum_w\\chi(C_w).\n\\]\nThen there is a flat projective \\(R\\)-scheme \\(\\mathcal Y\\) of finite presentation with\n\\[\n\\mathcal Y_L\\simeq Y,\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal Y_F\\simeq\\coprod_{w\\in\\mathcal V}C_w.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Form\n\\[\nA=\\bigoplus_{j\\geq0}A_j,\n\\]\nwith the integral section modules from Lemma 17.2. Each piece is finite free, products are defined by the valuations, and \\(A_0=R\\), since \\(Y\\) is projective geometrically integral.</p><p>Put\n\\[\nC=\\coprod_wC_w,\n\\qquad\nB_j=\\bigoplus_wH^0(C_w,\\mathcal O_{C_w}(jD_w)),\n\\qquad\nB=\\bigoplus_{j\\geq0}B_j.\n\\]\nThe two assumed equalities make dimensions in (7.5) equal in every sufficiently high degree. Hence, for some \\(j_0\\),\n\\[\nA_j/\\mathfrak m_RA_j\\simeq B_j\n\\quad(j\\geq j_0),\n\\tag{7.18}\n\\]\ncompatibly with multiplication.</p><p>Choose homogeneous positive-degree generators \\(b_1,\\ldots,b_t\\) of \\(B\\) over \\(B_0\\), of degrees \\(d_i\\). Let \\(W\\) be a common multiple of the \\(d_i\\), and choose \\(N=qW\\geq j_0\\) with \\(q\\geq t\\). From a monomial remove blocks \\(b_i^{W/d_i}\\) until each remaining exponent is less than \\(W/d_i\\). The remainder has weight less than \\(tW\\). For a monomial of degree \\(kN\\), its remainder has weight \\(cW\\), with \\(0\\leq c&lt;t\\leq q\\). Combine it with \\(q-c\\) blocks, and group the others into groups of \\(q\\). Thus\n\\[\nF\\oplus\\bigoplus_{k\\geq1}B_{kN}\n\\]\nis generated over \\(F\\) by \\(B_N\\).\n\\tag{7.19}\n\\]\nCoefficients in \\(B_0\\), which may exceed \\(F\\), are absorbed into the first positive-degree factor.</p><p>Choose an \\(R\\)-basis of \\(A_N\\), giving\n\\[\nP=R[x_1,\\ldots,x_u]\\longrightarrow A^{(N)}\n=\\bigoplus_{k\\geq0}A_{kN}.\n\\]\nReduction is surjective in every degree by (7.18)–(7.19). Degreewise cokernels are finite, so Nakayama gives surjectivity.</p><p>Let \\(K_k=\\ker(P_k\\to A_{kN})\\). Since the target is free, this is a finite direct summand, and its reduction is the special relation space. The homogeneous special relation ideal is finitely generated in the Noetherian polynomial ring over \\(F\\). Lift its generators to \\(g_1,\\ldots,g_v\\) in the kernel and let \\(I=(g_1,\\ldots,g_v)\\). Each \\(K_k/I_k\\) is finite and has zero reduction; Nakayama gives \\(K_k=I_k\\). Thus\n\\[\nA^{(N)}\n\\simeq\nR[x_1,\\ldots,x_u]/(g_1,\\ldots,g_v)\n\\]\nis finitely presented, without requiring \\(R\\) Noetherian.</p><p>Set \\(\\mathcal Y=\\operatorname{Proj}A^{(N)}\\). It is projective of finite presentation. It is flat: the graded algebra is a direct sum of free modules; homogeneous localizations are filtered colimits of flat modules; their degree-zero parts are direct summands.</p><p>The generic fiber is the Proj of the Veronese ample section ring of \\(Y\\), hence \\(Y\\). The special fiber is the Proj of (7.19). Replacing degree-zero \\(F\\) by \\(B_0\\) does not change Proj: after localizing at a positive-degree \\(f\\), a missing constant \\(b\\in B_0\\) is \\((bf)/f\\). The ample section ring recovers \\(C\\), proving the special-fiber assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 20", "orig": "Proposition 7.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(J\\) be finite with \\(O_p(J)=1\\), put \\(m=|J|\\), and suppose \\( s\\geq\\max\\{1,h\\}, \\qquad p^{s-h}>m(h+\\log_p m). \\) There is no finite extension \\(L/K_s\\) with \\( [L:K_s]_p<p^h \\) over which \\(X=\\mathcal X_L\\) …", "statement": "<p>Let \\(J\\) be finite with \\(O_p(J)=1\\), put \\(m=|J|\\), and suppose\n\\[\ns\\geq\\max\\{1,h\\},\n\\qquad\np^{s-h}&gt;m(h+\\log_p m).\n\\]\nThere is no finite extension \\(L/K_s\\) with\n\\[\n[L:K_s]_p&lt;p^h\n\\]\nover which \\(X=\\mathcal X_L\\) admits a geometrically connected smooth projective \\(J\\)-Galois cover, with specified \\(J\\)-action over \\(L\\), branched only at the marked points and having ramification index divisible by \\(p\\) at every marked point.</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose such a cover exists. Proposition 17 gives residue curves with total degree \\(m\\). Proposition 18 gives\n\\[\n\\chi(Y)=\\sum_w\\chi(C_w),\n\\qquad\n|I_w|&gt;1,\n\\]\nwith \\(I_w\\) a \\(p\\)-group. Proposition 19 constructs a flat projective finitely presented model with special fiber \\(\\coprod_wC_w\\). Its generic fiber is geometrically integral, so Lemma 20.1 makes the special fiber connected.</p><p>Each \\(C_w\\) is nonempty; therefore \\(\\mathcal V\\) has one member. Its stabilizer is all of \\(J\\), making \\(I_w\\) a nontrivial normal \\(p\\)-subgroup of \\(J\\). This contradicts \\(O_p(J)=1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 20.1", "orig": "Lemma 7.6", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(R\\) be a valuation ring of a characteristic-zero field \\(L\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(R\\) be a valuation ring of a characteristic-zero field \\(L\\). If a flat projective \\(R\\)-scheme \\(\\mathcal U\\) of finite presentation has geometrically integral generic fiber, then its special fiber is geometrically connected.</p>", "proof": "<p>The coefficients of finitely many homogeneous projective equations generate a finite-type \\(\\mathbb Z\\)-subdomain \\(R_0\\subset R\\). Normalize it in its fraction field to obtain \\(R_1\\). This normalization is finite, so \\(R_1\\) is normal Noetherian. It remains inside \\(R\\), since its elements are integral over \\(R\\), which is integrally closed.</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal U_1/R_1\\) be the scheme defined by the equations, and let \\(\\mathcal T\\) be the scheme-theoretic closure of its generic fiber. That generic fiber is geometrically integral because this can be checked after extension to \\(L\\). The closure \\(\\mathcal T\\) is integral and projective.</p><p>The closed subscheme \\(\\mathcal T_R\\subseteq\\mathcal U\\) agrees with \\(\\mathcal U\\) over \\(L\\). Its defining ideal is therefore torsion over \\(R\\); flatness makes the affine coordinate rings torsion-free. Hence\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_R=\\mathcal U.\n\\tag{7.20}\n\\]</p><p>Write \\(K_1=\\operatorname{Frac}(R_1)\\). Proper coherent finiteness gives a finite algebra \\(H^0(\\mathcal T,\\mathcal O_{\\mathcal T})\\). Restriction to the dense generic fiber is injective, and the latter's global functions are \\(K_1\\). Thus\n\\[\nR_1\\subseteq H^0(\\mathcal T,\\mathcal O_{\\mathcal T})\\subseteq K_1.\n\\]\nNormality gives equality with \\(R_1\\). On principal affine opens, flat cohomology base change identifies this with\n\\[\n\\pi_*\\mathcal O_{\\mathcal T}=\\mathcal O_{\\operatorname{Spec}R_1}.\n\\]\nNoetherian Stein connectedness gives geometrically connected fibers. By (7.20), the special fiber of \\(\\mathcal U\\) is a field extension of one of them, hence geometrically connected. All coherent-finiteness and Stein arguments occur over \\(R_1\\), not the original nondiscrete valuation ring.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 21", "orig": "Theorem 3.1, Uniform divisibility for generating tuples", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Let \\(J\\) be a finite group with \\(O_p(J)=1\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(J\\) be a finite group with \\(O_p(J)=1\\). For \\(s\\geq0\\), put \\(n=p^s\\). For a multiset \\(M\\) of \\(n\\) conjugacy classes of \\(p\\)-singular elements, let \\(N_{J,s}(M)\\) count, modulo simultaneous \\(J\\)-conjugation, ordered tuples\n\\[\n(x,y,u_1,\\ldots,u_n)\\in J^{n+2}\n\\]\nsatisfying\n\\[\n[x,y]u_1\\cdots u_n=1,\n\\qquad\n\\langle x,y,u_1,\\ldots,u_n\\rangle=J,\n\\qquad\n\\{[u_1],\\ldots,[u_n]\\}=M.\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\nFor every \\(h\\geq1\\), there is \\(s_0=s_0(p,J,h)\\) such that\n\\[\np^h\\mid N_{J,s}(M)\n\\quad(s\\geq s_0)\n\\]\nfor every \\(M\\). The entries remain ordered; empty sets have count zero.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(m=|J|\\), and choose \\(s_0\\) satisfying\n\\[\ns_0\\geq\\max\\{1,h\\},\n\\qquad\np^{s_0-h}&gt;m(h+\\log_p m).\n\\]\nThe same inequalities hold for all \\(s\\geq s_0\\).</p><p>Lemma 15.1 identifies the tuple-class set for any multiset \\(M\\) with \\(\\mathcal T_M\\). Proposition 15 supplies a finite Sylow \\(p\\)-group action. An orbit of size less than \\(p^h\\) would descend a cover over an extension \\(L/K_s\\) with \\([L:K_s]_p&lt;p^h\\), contrary to Proposition 20.</p><p>Every orbit has \\(p\\)-power size, hence every orbit size is divisible by \\(p^h\\). Their sum is therefore divisible by \\(p^h\\). The threshold depends only on \\(p,h,m\\), not on \\(M\\). Empty tuple sets, including those for groups without \\(p\\)-singular elements, satisfy the assertion.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [0, 3, "cite"], [0, 4, "cite"], [5, 0, "cite"], [5, 6, "cite"], [6, 0, "cite"], [6, 9, "cite"], [6, 12, "cite"], [6, 15, "cite"], [6, 17, "cite"], [7, 6, "cite"], [7, 8, "cite"], [7, 15, "cite"], [8, 6, "cite"], [9, 14, "cite"], [9, 15, "cite"], [9, 16, "cite"], [10, 9, "cite"], [10, 11, "cite"], [11, 6, "cite"], [11, 9, "cite"], [12, 17, "cite"], [13, 12, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [13, 18, "cite"], [14, 12, "cite"], [15, 18, "cite"], [16, 15, "cite"], [16, 18, "cite"], [17, 0, "cite"], [18, 17, "cite"], [19, 17, "cite"], [20, 17, "cite"], [21, 25, "cite"], [21, 26, "cite"], [21, 27, "cite"], [21, 33, "cite"], [21, 35, "cite"], [22, 21, "cite"], [23, 21, "cite"], [24, 21, "cite"], [25, 33, "cite"], [25, 34, "cite"], [26, 25, "cite"], [27, 25, "cite"], [28, 32, "cite"], [28, 33, "cite"], [28, 34, "cite"], [28, 42, "cite"], [28, 45, "cite"], [29, 28, "cite"], [29, 30, "cite"], [29, 31, "cite"], [30, 28, "cite"], [30, 31, "cite"], [31, 28, "cite"], [32, 33, "cite"], [33, 2, "cite"], [33, 34, "cite"], [33, 45, "cite"], [34, 2, "cite"], [34, 45, "cite"], [35, 36, "cite"], [35, 39, "cite"], [36, 39, "cite"], [36, 41, "cite"], [36, 45, "cite"], [37, 36, "cite"], [37, 44, "cite"], [38, 36, "cite"], [38, 42, "cite"], [39, 49, "cite"], [40, 49, "cite"], [41, 43, "cite"], [41, 45, "cite"], [42, 45, "cite"], [42, 46, "cite"], [42, 47, "cite"], [43, 42, "cite"], [43, 44, "cite"], [44, 42, "cite"], [44, 45, "cite"], [44, 46, "cite"], [45, 47, "cite"], [46, 47, "cite"], [47, 49, "cite"], [48, 47, "cite"], [49, 0, "cite"]]});
